WEBVTT
X-TIMESTAMP-MAP=LOCAL:00:00:00.000,MPEGTS:0

00:00.396 --> 00:03.986 align:middle
Transcript of Chair Powell's
Press Conference June 18,

00:04.596 --> 00:06.986 align:middle
2025 CHAIR POWELL.

00:07.126 --> 00:07.806 align:middle
Good afternoon.

00:08.026 --> 00:10.106 align:middle
My colleagues and I
remain squarely focused

00:10.106 --> 00:13.376 align:middle
on achieving our dual-mandate
goals of maximum employment

00:13.376 --> 00:16.646 align:middle
and stable prices for the
benefit of the American people.

00:17.736 --> 00:19.216 align:middle
Despite elevated uncertainty,

00:19.216 --> 00:21.336 align:middle
the economy is in
a solid position.

00:22.256 --> 00:25.446 align:middle
The unemployment rate remains
low, and the labor market is at

00:25.446 --> 00:27.096 align:middle
or near maximum employment.

00:28.156 --> 00:30.106 align:middle
Inflation has come
down a great deal

00:30.256 --> 00:31.706 align:middle
but has been running somewhat

00:31.706 --> 00:34.156 align:middle
above our 2 percent
longer-run objective.

00:34.796 --> 00:36.856 align:middle
In support of our goals,

00:36.856 --> 00:39.166 align:middle
today the Federal Open
Market Committee decided

00:39.166 --> 00:41.626 align:middle
to leave our policy
interest rate unchanged.

00:42.666 --> 00:44.066 align:middle
We believe that the
current stance

00:44.066 --> 00:47.726 align:middle
of monetary policy leaves us
well positioned to respond

00:47.726 --> 00:50.506 align:middle
in a timely way to potential
economic developments.

00:51.766 --> 00:53.776 align:middle
I'll have more to say
about monetary policy

00:53.776 --> 00:56.166 align:middle
after briefly reviewing
economic developments.

00:58.036 --> 01:02.376 align:middle
Following growth of 2.5 percent
last year, GDP was reported

01:02.376 --> 01:05.996 align:middle
to have edged down in the first
quarter, reflecting swings

01:06.066 --> 01:08.786 align:middle
in net exports that were
driven by businesses bringing

01:08.786 --> 01:11.116 align:middle
in imports ahead of
potential tariffs.

01:12.566 --> 01:16.006 align:middle
This unusual swing has
complicated GDP measurement.

01:16.846 --> 01:19.076 align:middle
Private domestic final
purchases, or PDFP,

01:19.076 --> 01:22.826 align:middle
as we call them-which
excludes net exports,

01:22.866 --> 01:25.946 align:middle
inventory investment, and
government spending-grew

01:25.946 --> 01:28.006 align:middle
at a solid 2.5 percent rate.

01:29.236 --> 01:32.716 align:middle
Within PDFP, growth of
consumer spending moderated,

01:32.896 --> 01:35.596 align:middle
while investment in equipment
and intangibles rebounded

01:36.026 --> 01:37.626 align:middle
from weakness in
the fourth quarter.

01:39.146 --> 01:41.306 align:middle
Surveys of households
and businesses, however,

01:41.396 --> 01:44.026 align:middle
report a decline in
sentiment over recent months

01:44.326 --> 01:46.846 align:middle
and elevated uncertainty
about the economic outlook,

01:47.576 --> 01:50.026 align:middle
largely reflecting
trade policy concerns.

01:50.576 --> 01:54.436 align:middle
It remains to be seen how these
developments might affect future

01:54.436 --> 01:55.536 align:middle
spending and investment.

01:57.326 --> 01:59.106 align:middle
In our Summary of
Economic Projections,

01:59.216 --> 02:01.806 align:middle
the median participant
projects GDP

02:01.806 --> 02:04.066 align:middle
to rise 1.4 percent this year

02:04.376 --> 02:07.586 align:middle
and 1.6 percent next
year-somewhat slower

02:07.586 --> 02:08.736 align:middle
than projected in March.

02:09.316 --> 02:12.706 align:middle
In the labor market,
conditions have remained solid.

02:13.756 --> 02:17.216 align:middle
Payroll job gains
averaged 135,000 per month

02:17.216 --> 02:18.476 align:middle
over the past three months.

02:19.676 --> 02:22.766 align:middle
The unemployment rate, at
4.2 percent, remains low

02:23.006 --> 02:25.346 align:middle
and has stayed in a narrow
range for the past year.

02:26.766 --> 02:28.416 align:middle
Wage growth has continued

02:28.416 --> 02:30.916 align:middle
to moderate while still
outpacing inflation.

02:32.306 --> 02:34.946 align:middle
Overall, a wide set
of indicators suggests

02:34.946 --> 02:37.626 align:middle
that conditions in the labor
market are broadly in balance

02:38.016 --> 02:39.866 align:middle
and consistent with
maximum employment.

02:41.166 --> 02:43.246 align:middle
The labor market is not a source

02:43.326 --> 02:45.846 align:middle
of significant inflationary
pressures.

02:46.376 --> 02:49.816 align:middle
The median projection
for the unemployment rate

02:49.866 --> 02:53.676 align:middle
in the SEP is 4.5 percent at
the end of this year and next,

02:53.886 --> 02:55.636 align:middle
a bit higher than
projected in March.

02:57.746 --> 03:00.256 align:middle
Inflation has eased
significantly from its highs

03:00.256 --> 03:04.156 align:middle
in mid-2022 but remains
somewhat elevated relative

03:04.156 --> 03:05.926 align:middle
to our 2 percent
longer-run goal.

03:07.116 --> 03:09.136 align:middle
Estimates based on the
consumer price index

03:09.136 --> 03:10.316 align:middle
and other data indicate

03:10.316 --> 03:13.386 align:middle
that total PCE prices
rose 2.3 percent

03:13.806 --> 03:15.286 align:middle
over the 12 months ending in May

03:16.176 --> 03:18.076 align:middle
and that-excluding
the volatile food

03:18.076 --> 03:22.886 align:middle
and energy categories-core
PCE prices rose 2.6 percent.

03:24.036 --> 03:26.996 align:middle
Near-term measures of inflation
expectations have moved

03:26.996 --> 03:29.826 align:middle
up over recent months, as
reflected in both market-

03:29.906 --> 03:31.456 align:middle
and survey-based measures.

03:32.786 --> 03:35.276 align:middle
Respondents to surveys
of consumers, businesses,

03:35.276 --> 03:37.976 align:middle
and professional
forecasters point to tariffs

03:38.036 --> 03:39.056 align:middle
as the driving factor.

03:39.426 --> 03:43.006 align:middle
Beyond the next year or
so, however, most measures

03:43.006 --> 03:45.406 align:middle
of longer-term expectations
remain consistent

03:45.406 --> 03:47.146 align:middle
with our 2 percent
inflation goal.

03:48.666 --> 03:50.306 align:middle
The median projection in the SEP

03:50.466 --> 03:54.056 align:middle
for total PCE inflation
this year is-is 3 percent,

03:54.386 --> 03:56.326 align:middle
somewhat higher than
projected in March.

03:57.766 --> 04:01.756 align:middle
The median inflation projection
falls to 2.4 percent in 2026

04:02.076 --> 04:05.076 align:middle
and 2.1 percent in 2027.

04:07.206 --> 04:10.566 align:middle
Our monetary policy actions
are guided by our dual mandate

04:10.626 --> 04:12.986 align:middle
to promote maximum
employment and stable prices

04:12.986 --> 04:13.956 align:middle
for the American people.

04:15.106 --> 04:16.716 align:middle
At today's meeting,
the Committee decided

04:16.716 --> 04:20.276 align:middle
to maintain the target range for
the federal funds rate at 4¼

04:20.276 --> 04:23.596 align:middle
to 4½ percent and to
continue reducing the size

04:23.596 --> 04:24.476 align:middle
of our balance sheet.

04:25.816 --> 04:27.916 align:middle
We will continue to determine
the appropriate stance

04:27.916 --> 04:30.106 align:middle
of monetary policy based
on the incoming data,

04:30.386 --> 04:32.616 align:middle
the evolving outlook,
and the balance of risks.

04:34.326 --> 04:36.546 align:middle
Changes to trade,
immigration, fiscal,

04:36.546 --> 04:39.986 align:middle
and regulatory policies continue
to evolve, and their effects

04:39.986 --> 04:41.746 align:middle
on the economy remain uncertain.

04:43.086 --> 04:45.686 align:middle
The effects of tariffs will
depend, among other things,

04:45.766 --> 04:46.786 align:middle
on their ultimate level.

04:47.676 --> 04:49.016 align:middle
Expectations of that level,

04:49.016 --> 04:52.386 align:middle
and thus of the related economic
effects, reached a peak in April

04:52.476 --> 04:53.736 align:middle
and have since declined.

04:54.956 --> 04:59.256 align:middle
Even so, increases in tariffs
this year are likely to push

04:59.256 --> 05:01.646 align:middle
up prices and weigh
on economic activity.

05:03.066 --> 05:05.726 align:middle
The effects on inflation could
be short lived-reflecting a

05:05.726 --> 05:07.536 align:middle
one-time shift in
the price level.

05:08.486 --> 05:10.966 align:middle
It's also possible that the
inflationary effects could

05:10.966 --> 05:12.196 align:middle
instead be more persistent.

05:12.956 --> 05:14.986 align:middle
Avoiding that outcome
will depend on the size

05:14.986 --> 05:17.806 align:middle
of the tariff effects, on how
long it takes for them to pass

05:17.806 --> 05:20.146 align:middle
through fully into
prices, and, ultimately,

05:20.226 --> 05:23.066 align:middle
on keeping longer-term inflation
expectations well anchored.

05:24.206 --> 05:26.956 align:middle
Our obligation is to keep
longer-term inflation

05:26.956 --> 05:29.936 align:middle
expectations well anchored and
to prevent a one-time increase

05:29.936 --> 05:33.066 align:middle
in the price level from becoming
an ongoing inflation problem.

05:34.486 --> 05:36.076 align:middle
As we act to meet
that obligation,

05:36.766 --> 05:38.646 align:middle
we will balance our
maximum-employment

05:38.706 --> 05:41.456 align:middle
and price-stability mandates,
keeping in mind that,

05:41.456 --> 05:44.576 align:middle
without price stability, we
cannot achieve the long periods

05:44.616 --> 05:46.236 align:middle
of strong labor market
conditions

05:46.636 --> 05:48.336 align:middle
that benefit all Americans.

05:49.986 --> 05:52.146 align:middle
We may find ourselves in
the challenging scenario

05:52.146 --> 05:54.426 align:middle
in which our dual-mandate
goals are in tension.

05:55.086 --> 05:58.546 align:middle
If that were to occur, we would
consider how far the economy is

05:58.686 --> 06:01.416 align:middle
from each goal and the
potentially different time

06:01.416 --> 06:04.376 align:middle
horizons over which those
respective gaps would be

06:04.376 --> 06:05.566 align:middle
anticipated to close.

06:07.006 --> 06:10.416 align:middle
For the time being, we are well
positioned to wait to learn more

06:10.516 --> 06:11.496 align:middle
about the likely course

06:11.496 --> 06:14.296 align:middle
of the economy before
considering any adjustments

06:14.296 --> 06:15.376 align:middle
to our policy stance.

06:17.206 --> 06:20.256 align:middle
In our SEP, FOMC
participants wrote

06:20.256 --> 06:21.706 align:middle
down their individual
assessments

06:21.706 --> 06:24.236 align:middle
of an appropriate path for
the federal funds rate,

06:24.726 --> 06:26.746 align:middle
based on what each
participant judges

06:26.776 --> 06:29.066 align:middle
to be the most likely
scenario going forward.

06:30.756 --> 06:33.226 align:middle
The median projection
projects-participant-median

06:33.226 --> 06:35.276 align:middle
participant projects that
the appropriate level

06:35.276 --> 06:38.056 align:middle
of the federal funds rate
will be 3.9 percent at the end

06:38.056 --> 06:40.566 align:middle
of this year, the same
as projected in March.

06:41.046 --> 06:44.866 align:middle
The median projection declines
to 3.6 percent at the end

06:44.866 --> 06:48.826 align:middle
of next year and to 3.4
percent at the end of 2027,

06:49.456 --> 06:51.016 align:middle
a little higher than
the March projection.

06:52.076 --> 06:55.766 align:middle
These individual forecasts are
always subject to uncertainty,

06:55.926 --> 06:57.256 align:middle
and, as I have noted,

06:58.296 --> 07:00.076 align:middle
uncertainty is unusually
elevated.

07:00.676 --> 07:01.186 align:middle
And, of course,

07:01.186 --> 07:03.946 align:middle
these projections are not a
Committee plan or decision.

07:05.086 --> 07:08.396 align:middle
At this meeting, the Committee
continued its discussions

07:08.396 --> 07:09.846 align:middle
as part of our five-year review

07:09.846 --> 07:11.426 align:middle
of our monetary policy
framework.

07:12.636 --> 07:15.056 align:middle
We focused on issues related
to assessing the risks

07:15.056 --> 07:17.566 align:middle
and uncertainties that are
relevant for monetary policy

07:17.976 --> 07:19.226 align:middle
and the potential implications

07:19.226 --> 07:21.696 align:middle
for policy strategy
and communications.

07:22.856 --> 07:24.376 align:middle
Our review includes outreach

07:24.376 --> 07:26.846 align:middle
and public events involving
a wide range of parties,

07:27.026 --> 07:29.246 align:middle
including Fed Listens
events around the country

07:29.706 --> 07:31.936 align:middle
and a research conference
that we held last month.

07:33.256 --> 07:35.416 align:middle
We are open to new ideas
and critical feedback,

07:35.696 --> 07:38.606 align:middle
and we will take on board
lessons of the last five years

07:39.026 --> 07:40.306 align:middle
in determining our findings.

07:41.546 --> 07:44.136 align:middle
We intend to wrap up any
modifications to our Statement

07:44.136 --> 07:47.556 align:middle
on Longer-Run Goals and Monetary
Policy Strategy by late summer.

07:48.396 --> 07:50.996 align:middle
After that, we will consider
enhancements to our suite

07:50.996 --> 07:53.426 align:middle
of communications
tools, including the SEP.

07:55.576 --> 07:58.366 align:middle
The Fed has been assigned two
goals for monetary policy:

07:58.526 --> 08:00.786 align:middle
maximum employment
and stable prices.

08:01.756 --> 08:04.256 align:middle
We remain committed to
supporting maximum employment,

08:04.676 --> 08:07.536 align:middle
bringing inflation sustainably
to our 2 percent goal,

08:07.936 --> 08:10.236 align:middle
and keeping longer-term
inflation expectations

08:10.306 --> 08:10.866 align:middle
well anchored.

08:11.726 --> 08:12.926 align:middle
Our success in delivering

08:12.926 --> 08:15.346 align:middle
on these goals matters
to all Americans.

08:15.896 --> 08:18.496 align:middle
We understand that our actions
affect communities, families,

08:18.496 --> 08:20.056 align:middle
and businesses across
the country.

08:20.956 --> 08:23.376 align:middle
Everything we do is in
service to our public mission.

08:24.366 --> 08:26.446 align:middle
And we at the Fed will
do everything we can

08:26.506 --> 08:28.486 align:middle
to achieve our
maximum-employment

08:28.486 --> 08:29.726 align:middle
and price-stability goals.

08:30.526 --> 08:30.876 align:middle
Thank you.

08:30.986 --> 08:32.546 align:middle
I look forward to
your questions.

08:32.736 --> 08:33.116 align:middle
MICHELLE SMITH.

08:33.116 --> 08:35.866 align:middle
Colby. COLBY SMITH.

08:36.136 --> 08:36.556 align:middle
Thank you.

08:36.606 --> 08:37.876 align:middle
Colby Smith with
the New York Times.

08:38.196 --> 08:40.986 align:middle
To what extent has the more
limited impact from tariffs

08:40.986 --> 08:43.876 align:middle
at this stage on
inflation changed your view

08:44.116 --> 08:46.336 align:middle
on what the ultimate
economic fallout will be

08:46.336 --> 08:48.456 align:middle
from these policies
and the timing

08:48.456 --> 08:50.426 align:middle
of when they will
materialize in the data?

08:51.766 --> 08:52.806 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

08:53.826 --> 08:58.276 align:middle
So we've had three months of,
of favorable inflation readings

08:58.276 --> 09:00.066 align:middle
since the high readings
of January and February,

09:00.066 --> 09:01.696 align:middle
and that's, of course,
highly welcome news.

09:02.266 --> 09:04.646 align:middle
Part of that just
is that services,

09:04.646 --> 09:07.206 align:middle
core services-both
housing services

09:07.206 --> 09:09.956 align:middle
and nonhousing services
have really been grinding

09:09.956 --> 09:12.206 align:middle
down toward levels that
are-that are consistent

09:12.206 --> 09:13.156 align:middle
with 2 percent inflation.

09:13.156 --> 09:13.996 align:middle
So that's the good news.

09:14.506 --> 09:19.526 align:middle
We've had goods inflation just
moving up a bit, and, of course,

09:19.526 --> 09:22.266 align:middle
we expect-as you-as you
point out, we, we do expect

09:22.576 --> 09:25.736 align:middle
to see more of that over
the course of the summer.

09:25.856 --> 09:27.926 align:middle
It takes some time for
tariffs to work their way

09:27.926 --> 09:28.436 align:middle
through the chain

09:28.436 --> 09:30.076 align:middle
of distribution to
the end consumer.

09:30.626 --> 09:33.516 align:middle
A good example of that
would be, goods being sold

09:33.516 --> 09:35.986 align:middle
at retailers today may have
been imported several months

09:35.986 --> 09:39.096 align:middle
ago-before tariffs were
imposed-so we're beginning

09:39.096 --> 09:39.996 align:middle
to see some effects.

09:39.996 --> 09:42.776 align:middle
And we do expect to see more
of them over coming months.

09:43.216 --> 09:45.676 align:middle
We do-we do also see
price increases in some

09:45.676 --> 09:48.796 align:middle
of the relevant categories
like personal computers

09:48.796 --> 09:50.606 align:middle
and audio-visual
equipment-things like that

09:51.226 --> 09:53.276 align:middle
that are attributable
to tariff increases.

09:53.716 --> 09:56.996 align:middle
In addition, we look at surveys
of businesses, and there,

09:56.996 --> 09:58.246 align:middle
there are many of those.

09:58.246 --> 10:02.526 align:middle
And, and, you, you do see a
range of things, but, but many,

10:02.526 --> 10:07.786 align:middle
many companies do expect to-to,
to put all or-some or all

10:07.786 --> 10:09.816 align:middle
of the effect of tariffs
through to the next,

10:09.906 --> 10:12.006 align:middle
next person in the-in
the chain and,

10:12.006 --> 10:13.196 align:middle
ultimately, to the consumer.

10:14.216 --> 10:16.406 align:middle
Today-you know, the
amount of these-the,

10:16.406 --> 10:17.646 align:middle
the amount of the
tariff effects,

10:17.646 --> 10:20.716 align:middle
the size of the tariff
effects, their duration,

10:20.716 --> 10:22.966 align:middle
and the time it will take
are all highly uncertain.

10:22.966 --> 10:27.056 align:middle
So that, that is why we think
the appropriate thing to do is

10:27.056 --> 10:29.336 align:middle
to hold where we are
as we learn more,

10:29.726 --> 10:31.326 align:middle
and we think our
policy stance is,

10:31.376 --> 10:33.646 align:middle
is in a good place-where
we're well positioned to react

10:33.646 --> 10:34.636 align:middle
to incoming developments.

10:35.446 --> 10:35.876 align:middle
COLBY SMITH.

10:35.876 --> 10:38.366 align:middle
So in terms of how we should
interpret the rate cuts penciled

10:38.366 --> 10:42.756 align:middle
into the SEP, is this reflecting
that there's this expectation

10:42.756 --> 10:45.636 align:middle
that underlying inflation will
just stay well enough contained

10:45.636 --> 10:46.706 align:middle
that allows the Committee

10:46.706 --> 10:49.716 align:middle
to eventually move
ahead with those cuts?

10:49.716 --> 10:52.126 align:middle
Or is it about, you know,
responding to a deterioration

10:52.126 --> 10:53.996 align:middle
in economic activity, let's say?

10:54.226 --> 10:55.966 align:middle
I mean, how should we make
sense of the forecast?

10:56.016 --> 10:56.306 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

10:56.306 --> 10:57.906 align:middle
So if you look at the
forecast, you will see

10:57.906 --> 11:00.686 align:middle
that people do generally
expect inflation to move up

11:00.686 --> 11:02.606 align:middle
and then to come back down.

11:02.766 --> 11:04.626 align:middle
But we can't just assume that.

11:04.626 --> 11:06.936 align:middle
Of course, we don't know
that, and, you know, our,

11:07.006 --> 11:09.716 align:middle
our job is to make sure-one
of our jobs-to make sure

11:09.716 --> 11:12.876 align:middle
that a one-time increase
in inflation doesn't turn

11:12.876 --> 11:15.196 align:middle
into an inflation problem.

11:15.286 --> 11:18.016 align:middle
And that, again-that will depend
on the size of the effects,

11:18.226 --> 11:20.446 align:middle
how long it takes for them to
come in, and, and, ultimately,

11:20.446 --> 11:22.976 align:middle
on, on keeping inflation
expectations anchored.

11:23.896 --> 11:24.346 align:middle
MICHELLE SMITH.

11:24.956 --> 11:28.416 align:middle
Howard. HOWARD SCHNEIDER.

11:28.416 --> 11:29.936 align:middle
Hi, and thanks, Chair Powell.

11:29.936 --> 11:30.976 align:middle
Howard Schneider with Reuters.

11:31.346 --> 11:34.646 align:middle
If you look at the, the rate
path starting in December

11:34.646 --> 11:39.776 align:middle
to today and adjust it over the
full time horizon you've got

11:39.776 --> 11:41.846 align:middle
there, you've taken
about ¼ point per year

11:42.416 --> 11:43.736 align:middle
out of your projected path.

11:43.736 --> 11:46.206 align:middle
And you end at a
higher rate in end-2027

11:46.206 --> 11:48.626 align:middle
than you were-would have
in the prior forecast.

11:49.336 --> 11:52.846 align:middle
Is that a result of, of a
sense that tariffs will lead

11:52.846 --> 11:54.466 align:middle
to more persistent inflation?

11:54.946 --> 11:56.546 align:middle
Is it a result of reassessments

11:56.546 --> 11:58.266 align:middle
of where your short-term
neutral rate is?

11:58.266 --> 11:59.806 align:middle
Is it-why, why are you
on a slower path now?

11:59.876 --> 12:00.706 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

12:01.006 --> 12:04.216 align:middle
So I would focus most on
the-on the nearer term.

12:04.216 --> 12:08.296 align:middle
As you think-as you get out
to the-to the later years,

12:08.296 --> 12:10.996 align:middle
it's hard to-it's hard
for anybody to know

12:10.996 --> 12:12.036 align:middle
where the economy is going.

12:12.036 --> 12:14.736 align:middle
You didn't see people moving
their longer-term, you know,

12:14.736 --> 12:17.956 align:middle
estimate of the neutral rate,
for example, at this meeting.

12:17.956 --> 12:20.366 align:middle
So-and those things are
probably slow moving.

12:20.366 --> 12:22.816 align:middle
So I think-I think if you
look at what's happening here

12:23.156 --> 12:24.726 align:middle
since March-this is since March,

12:24.726 --> 12:27.976 align:middle
right?-you see a little slower
growth, just a tiny tick up,

12:28.066 --> 12:31.286 align:middle
one-tenth tick up,
in, in unemployment,

12:31.286 --> 12:33.226 align:middle
and you see inflation
moving up three-tenths.

12:33.416 --> 12:35.196 align:middle
And by the way, it
was-it was a similar move

12:35.586 --> 12:38.696 align:middle
from the December
SEP to the March.

12:38.996 --> 12:40.286 align:middle
So that's what you see.

12:40.386 --> 12:42.446 align:middle
You see the, the
effects of tariffs.

12:42.746 --> 12:45.646 align:middle
I think we learned in April,
after the March meeting,

12:46.016 --> 12:49.746 align:middle
that substantially higher
tariffs were likely,

12:49.746 --> 12:51.686 align:middle
and then since then,
the estimates

12:51.686 --> 12:53.616 align:middle
of where the tariffs will be
have actually moved back down,

12:53.616 --> 12:55.166 align:middle
although still at
an elevated level.

12:55.166 --> 12:59.356 align:middle
So we're adapting in real time,
and what you see is, you know,

12:59.406 --> 13:01.486 align:middle
an, an accumulation of
individual assessments.

13:01.546 --> 13:02.036 align:middle
HOWARD SCHNEIDER.

13:02.136 --> 13:04.076 align:middle
Okay, but you say
in the statement

13:04.076 --> 13:08.186 align:middle
that risks have diminished on
that front, but the July 9,

13:08.406 --> 13:10.426 align:middle
you know, drop-dead
date for all the,

13:10.526 --> 13:13.436 align:middle
the Liberation Day tariffs is
still out there and unresolved.

13:13.436 --> 13:16.506 align:middle
You've got now an
exchange of missiles

13:16.506 --> 13:18.266 align:middle
between two Middle
East adversaries,

13:18.266 --> 13:21.046 align:middle
with a possible U.S.
involvement-how can you justify

13:21.046 --> 13:22.356 align:middle
saying that risks
have diminished?

13:22.356 --> 13:22.816 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

13:23.296 --> 13:27.576 align:middle
So what we said was that
uncertainty has, has-uncertainty

13:27.576 --> 13:30.076 align:middle
about the economic outlook has
diminished but remains elevated.

13:30.416 --> 13:31.606 align:middle
Many, many surveys say that.

13:31.926 --> 13:34.796 align:middle
They do. So-and, and,
that's-that's actually a line

13:34.796 --> 13:36.766 align:middle
from the Tealbook, which
you can see in five years.

13:36.986 --> 13:38.406 align:middle
Remember to check that.

13:38.406 --> 13:39.006 align:middle
[Laughter] HOWARD SCHNEIDER.

13:39.486 --> 13:40.846 align:middle
Maybe the world will
be over by then.

13:41.256 --> 13:41.776 align:middle
[Laughter] CHAIR POWELL.

13:42.286 --> 13:43.616 align:middle
No-but if you think of that,

13:43.716 --> 13:47.506 align:middle
tariff uncertainty-uncertainty
really peaked in April

13:47.796 --> 13:49.286 align:middle
and since then has come down.

13:49.286 --> 13:51.346 align:middle
And that's-that's really what
that's just acknowledging.

13:51.346 --> 13:54.356 align:middle
It's diminished but still
elevated-that it's uncertainty.

13:54.436 --> 13:56.056 align:middle
So I think that's an
accurate statement.

13:56.506 --> 13:56.926 align:middle
MICHELLE SMITH.

13:57.416 --> 13:58.146 align:middle
Chris Rugaber.

13:58.476 --> 13:59.096 align:middle
CHRISTOPHER RUGABER.

14:00.536 --> 14:00.966 align:middle
Thank you.

14:00.966 --> 14:02.586 align:middle
Chris Rugaber at
Associated Press.

14:02.766 --> 14:05.716 align:middle
There is an argument
out there in favor

14:05.716 --> 14:09.146 align:middle
of cutting rates
more immediately.

14:09.556 --> 14:12.046 align:middle
Inflation has continued
to cool and is back

14:12.046 --> 14:14.476 align:middle
at roughly 2 percent,
despite the tariffs.

14:14.476 --> 14:15.976 align:middle
And I guess I also wanted
to ask about, you know,

14:15.976 --> 14:17.506 align:middle
cracks in the job market,

14:17.606 --> 14:20.656 align:middle
with gross hiring
slowing-concentrated

14:20.656 --> 14:22.026 align:middle
in just a few industries.

14:22.436 --> 14:25.866 align:middle
We've seen some housing
data, including this morning,

14:25.866 --> 14:26.906 align:middle
that have been pretty weak.

14:27.456 --> 14:31.466 align:middle
Do you see any concerns
that, you know,

14:31.466 --> 14:33.176 align:middle
the economy is weakening
and that is a reason

14:33.176 --> 14:35.616 align:middle
to cut rates going forward?

14:36.046 --> 14:36.696 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

14:36.696 --> 14:39.106 align:middle
So we do-we do, of course,
monitor all those things.

14:39.656 --> 14:42.566 align:middle
I, I think if you look at the
overall picture, you know,

14:42.566 --> 14:45.056 align:middle
what you're seeing is
4.2 percent unemployment

14:45.166 --> 14:48.996 align:middle
and an economy that's growing
at a-at a rate hard to know,

14:48.996 --> 14:52.546 align:middle
given the, the unusual
flows in the first quarter.

14:52.546 --> 14:53.746 align:middle
But it appears to be 1½,

14:54.086 --> 14:55.996 align:middle
2 percent-maybe a
little better than that.

14:56.426 --> 14:59.816 align:middle
Sentiment has come up off
of its very low levels.

14:59.816 --> 15:01.436 align:middle
It's still-it's still depressed.

15:02.136 --> 15:04.536 align:middle
So, you know, you
can-you can point

15:04.536 --> 15:06.976 align:middle
to things-the housing market
is a longer-run problem

15:07.526 --> 15:08.976 align:middle
and also a short-run problem.

15:09.036 --> 15:12.486 align:middle
I don't think it's indicative
of-you know, basically,

15:12.486 --> 15:15.346 align:middle
the situation is, we have a
longer-run shortage of housing,

15:15.976 --> 15:18.256 align:middle
and we also have
high rates right now.

15:18.256 --> 15:21.046 align:middle
I think the best thing we can
do for the housing market is

15:21.046 --> 15:24.776 align:middle
to restore price stability
in a sustainable way and,

15:24.776 --> 15:26.256 align:middle
and create a strong
labor market,

15:26.316 --> 15:31.026 align:middle
and that's the best thing we
can do for the housing market.

15:31.026 --> 15:34.266 align:middle
You asked about the
job market-again,

15:34.366 --> 15:36.016 align:middle
look at labor force
participation.

15:36.016 --> 15:37.036 align:middle
Look at wages.

15:37.166 --> 15:38.456 align:middle
Look at job creation.

15:38.456 --> 15:40.166 align:middle
They're all at healthy
levels now.

15:40.626 --> 15:44.566 align:middle
I, I would say you can
see perhaps a very,

15:44.566 --> 15:46.296 align:middle
very slow continued cooling,

15:46.786 --> 15:48.616 align:middle
but nothing that's
troubling at this time.

15:48.676 --> 15:51.276 align:middle
But, you know, we watch it-we
watch it very, very carefully.

15:51.276 --> 15:53.606 align:middle
So, overall, again,
the current stance

15:53.606 --> 15:56.736 align:middle
of monetary policy leaves us
well positioned to respond

15:56.736 --> 15:58.586 align:middle
in a timely way to
economic developments,

15:58.586 --> 16:01.916 align:middle
and-for now-and we'll be
watching the data carefully.

16:01.916 --> 16:02.636 align:middle
CHRISTOPHER RUGABER.

16:02.726 --> 16:06.206 align:middle
Well, just quickly
on-given that, you know,

16:06.386 --> 16:08.456 align:middle
there are concerns inflation
will rise-but there is the

16:08.456 --> 16:12.876 align:middle
alternate scenario that tariffs
would create demand destruction

16:12.876 --> 16:16.336 align:middle
and slow growth sufficiently and
that would perhaps keep a bit

16:16.336 --> 16:17.286 align:middle
of a lid on inflation.

16:17.816 --> 16:20.336 align:middle
Do you see odds of
that scenario,

16:20.336 --> 16:22.766 align:middle
what kind of odds do you see
of that scenario coming true,

16:22.766 --> 16:25.556 align:middle
and how many months of cool
inflation would you need

16:25.556 --> 16:27.866 align:middle
to see before concluding
that maybe

16:27.866 --> 16:29.996 align:middle
that lower-inflation
scenario is taking place?

16:30.106 --> 16:30.576 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

16:30.576 --> 16:33.516 align:middle
So this is very much the
conversation we had today

16:33.516 --> 16:34.126 align:middle
and yesterday.

16:34.126 --> 16:34.806 align:middle
There, there are many,

16:34.806 --> 16:37.596 align:middle
many different scenarios-many
combinations of scenarios

16:37.596 --> 16:41.226 align:middle
where inflation does or
doesn't prove out to be

16:41.226 --> 16:43.356 align:middle
at the levels we think and
where the labor market does

16:43.356 --> 16:44.686 align:middle
or doesn't soften.

16:44.926 --> 16:48.906 align:middle
And I think what, what you see
people doing is looking ahead

16:48.906 --> 16:51.946 align:middle
at a time of very high
uncertainty and writing

16:51.946 --> 16:53.936 align:middle
down what they think
the most likely case is.

16:54.236 --> 16:57.936 align:middle
No one holds these, these
rate paths with a great deal

16:57.936 --> 17:00.096 align:middle
of conviction, and
everyone would agree

17:00.496 --> 17:02.176 align:middle
that they're all going
to be data dependent.

17:02.176 --> 17:06.356 align:middle
And that-you can make a case
for, for any of the rate paths,

17:06.356 --> 17:08.986 align:middle
I think, that you
see in, in the SEP.

17:09.276 --> 17:10.776 align:middle
And, you know, we do
this once a quarter.

17:11.316 --> 17:14.516 align:middle
It's-it's a hard thing to do at
this-particularly at this time.

17:14.516 --> 17:17.386 align:middle
But it does reflect-you know, if
you see somebody writing down,

17:17.536 --> 17:20.226 align:middle
you know, a, a rate
path that involves cuts,

17:20.226 --> 17:22.956 align:middle
that's them saying, "Yes, I
think we will get to a place,

17:23.286 --> 17:25.576 align:middle
more likely than not, where
cuts will be appropriate."

17:25.756 --> 17:28.086 align:middle
And it could be-it could
be a joint probability

17:28.086 --> 17:29.546 align:middle
of a number of possible
outcomes.

17:30.016 --> 17:32.166 align:middle
Again, remember how much
uncertainty we face, though.

17:32.736 --> 17:34.426 align:middle
MICHELLE SMITH.

17:34.976 --> 17:36.606 align:middle
Steve. STEVE LIESMAN.

17:36.606 --> 17:36.866 align:middle
Thank you.

17:37.056 --> 17:38.726 align:middle
Mr. Chair, I wonder
if you could describe

17:38.726 --> 17:39.816 align:middle
for us some of those scenarios.

17:39.816 --> 17:42.386 align:middle
How do you get to a
place-I'm noticing

17:42.386 --> 17:44.816 align:middle
that the uncertainty levels in
your forecast are very high.

17:45.376 --> 17:47.796 align:middle
How do you get to a place
where you have the confidence

17:47.796 --> 17:50.996 align:middle
in the outlook for-say,
inflation and/or growth

17:50.996 --> 17:53.766 align:middle
or the unemployment rate-how
many months does it take,

17:53.766 --> 17:56.166 align:middle
and what do you want to see in
the data to get to that level

17:56.166 --> 17:58.926 align:middle
of confidence to actually reduce
rates off the restrictive level?

17:58.926 --> 17:59.276 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

17:59.326 --> 18:01.276 align:middle
So it's-it's-again, it's
very, very hard to say

18:01.276 --> 18:02.366 align:middle
when that will happen.

18:02.366 --> 18:05.646 align:middle
We know that the time will come;
it come-could come quickly.

18:06.176 --> 18:07.306 align:middle
It could not come quickly.

18:07.696 --> 18:10.036 align:middle
As long as the economy
is solid, though-as long

18:10.036 --> 18:13.206 align:middle
as we're seeing the kind of
labor market that we have

18:13.206 --> 18:16.896 align:middle
and reasonably decent growth
and inflation moving down,

18:16.896 --> 18:20.396 align:middle
we feel like the right thing
to do is to be where we are,

18:20.396 --> 18:22.926 align:middle
with where our policy stance
is, and just learn more.

18:22.926 --> 18:24.786 align:middle
And in particular, we
feel like we're going

18:24.786 --> 18:27.926 align:middle
to learn a great deal more
over the summer on tariffs.

18:27.926 --> 18:31.616 align:middle
We do-we hadn't expected
them to show up much by now,

18:31.616 --> 18:34.466 align:middle
and they haven't, and we
will see whether-the extent

18:34.466 --> 18:36.526 align:middle
to which they do over,
over coming months.

18:36.526 --> 18:37.266 align:middle
And I think that's going

18:37.266 --> 18:39.836 align:middle
to inform our thinking,
for one thing.

18:39.836 --> 18:41.746 align:middle
In addition, we'll see how
the labor market progresses.

18:41.746 --> 18:43.916 align:middle
So, at some point,
it will become clear.

18:43.916 --> 18:45.616 align:middle
I can't tell you exactly
when that will be.

18:46.196 --> 18:48.026 align:middle
And, you know, meanwhile,
we'll be watching,

18:48.206 --> 18:50.986 align:middle
watching the labor market very
carefully for signs of weakness

18:50.986 --> 18:53.866 align:middle
and strength and
tariffs for signs of,

18:53.866 --> 18:55.196 align:middle
of what's going to happen there.

18:55.196 --> 18:57.726 align:middle
And, of course, there are many
developments ahead, you know,

18:57.726 --> 18:59.996 align:middle
even in the near
term-developments are expected

19:00.436 --> 19:00.926 align:middle
on tariffs.

19:00.926 --> 19:03.316 align:middle
So I think we, we don't yet
know with any confidence

19:03.316 --> 19:04.416 align:middle
where they will settle out.

19:04.416 --> 19:07.516 align:middle
We have an estimate, and it's a
pretty-I think all estimates are

19:07.516 --> 19:08.796 align:middle
now pretty close together.

19:09.336 --> 19:10.846 align:middle
But it's-it's, yeah,
highly uncertain.

19:11.426 --> 19:12.006 align:middle
STEVE LIESMAN.

19:12.006 --> 19:14.746 align:middle
When you say "estimate"-estimate
of the impact of tariffs

19:14.746 --> 19:17.166 align:middle
on the core PCE-is
that what it is?

19:17.166 --> 19:17.836 align:middle
And can you share that?

19:17.836 --> 19:18.126 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

19:18.126 --> 19:19.226 align:middle
Yeah, what you start with is,

19:19.306 --> 19:21.906 align:middle
is-what's the effective
tariff rate overall?

19:22.086 --> 19:23.726 align:middle
And people are managing to that.

19:24.476 --> 19:27.306 align:middle
But, you know, the, the
pass-through of tariffs

19:27.306 --> 19:31.136 align:middle
to consumer price inflation
is a whole process that's

19:31.136 --> 19:31.876 align:middle
very uncertain.

19:31.876 --> 19:34.326 align:middle
You know, as you know, there
are many parties in that chain:

19:34.326 --> 19:37.016 align:middle
There's the manufacturer,
the exporter, the importer,

19:37.016 --> 19:40.506 align:middle
the retailer, and the consumer,
and each one of those is going

19:40.506 --> 19:43.556 align:middle
to be trying not to be the
one to, to pay for the tariff.

19:43.626 --> 19:45.176 align:middle
But together, they will all pay

19:45.176 --> 19:48.136 align:middle
for it together-or maybe
one party will pay it all.

19:48.646 --> 19:52.366 align:middle
But that process is very hard
to predict, and we haven't been

19:52.366 --> 19:55.336 align:middle
through a situation like this,
and I think we have to be humble

19:55.336 --> 19:57.576 align:middle
about our ability
to forecast it.

19:57.576 --> 19:59.666 align:middle
So that's why we need
to see some actual data

19:59.666 --> 20:01.526 align:middle
to have-to make better
decisions.

20:01.526 --> 20:04.286 align:middle
We, we'd like to get some, some
more data, and, and, again,

20:04.286 --> 20:05.406 align:middle
in the meantime, we can do

20:05.406 --> 20:07.886 align:middle
that because the economy
remains in solid condition.

20:09.846 --> 20:10.536 align:middle
MICHELLE SMITH.

20:11.286 --> 20:14.816 align:middle
Nick. NICK TIMIRAOS.

20:14.976 --> 20:16.376 align:middle
Nick Timiraos of the
Wall Street Journal.

20:16.786 --> 20:18.726 align:middle
Chair Powell, I guess
I'm wondering

20:18.726 --> 20:22.126 align:middle
if you could explain a little
more the divergence we see

20:22.126 --> 20:23.646 align:middle
in the dot plot, particularly

20:23.646 --> 20:25.696 align:middle
around the 2025 rate
projections.

20:25.806 --> 20:29.046 align:middle
I realize this is-you know,
you have one group of officials

20:29.046 --> 20:32.016 align:middle
that are putting down no
cuts, another that are putting

20:32.596 --> 20:36.366 align:middle
down more than one-and
recognizing

20:36.366 --> 20:39.026 align:middle
that could be difficult to
summarize, but is it a matter

20:39.026 --> 20:41.026 align:middle
of people having a
different outlook

20:41.606 --> 20:43.776 align:middle
or a different reaction
function?

20:43.776 --> 20:45.506 align:middle
A different commitment
to defending

20:45.506 --> 20:47.226 align:middle
against another inflation
mistake?

20:47.596 --> 20:52.676 align:middle
How, how did that play out
over the last two days?

20:52.676 --> 20:53.296 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

20:53.296 --> 20:55.816 align:middle
So you're right, we-and,
as is often the case,

20:55.816 --> 20:57.316 align:middle
we have a pretty
healthy diversity

20:57.316 --> 20:58.276 align:middle
of views on the Committee.

20:58.876 --> 21:01.226 align:middle
We did have strong support
for today's decision

21:01.226 --> 21:03.776 align:middle
and broad agreement that our
policy stance does leave us

21:03.776 --> 21:04.466 align:middle
in a good place.

21:05.056 --> 21:07.776 align:middle
But I would point to two
factors, and you mentioned them.

21:07.776 --> 21:10.666 align:middle
The first is just that parties
have a diversity of forecasts,

21:11.166 --> 21:12.906 align:middle
and, and they do align with,

21:13.106 --> 21:14.666 align:middle
with where-with where
their dots are.

21:14.666 --> 21:17.176 align:middle
So if you have a higher
inflation forecast, you're going

21:17.176 --> 21:21.946 align:middle
to be less likely to be writing
down, you know, more, more cuts.

21:22.246 --> 21:25.026 align:middle
But as-remember, as we see more
data, we're going to learn more

21:25.026 --> 21:26.286 align:middle
about where inflation is headed.

21:26.286 --> 21:28.876 align:middle
And that means when
it is time to look at,

21:29.056 --> 21:31.126 align:middle
at normal-at sort of, at,

21:31.336 --> 21:33.916 align:middle
you know-resuming our
normalization process,

21:34.186 --> 21:36.576 align:middle
the differences you may-you
see should be smaller

21:36.576 --> 21:38.176 align:middle
because we'll have
seen actual data.

21:38.396 --> 21:40.816 align:middle
Right now, it's just a
forecast in a very foggy time.

21:41.216 --> 21:42.696 align:middle
So that's the first
part-is forecast.

21:42.966 --> 21:45.046 align:middle
Secondly, people can
look at the same data,

21:45.046 --> 21:47.816 align:middle
and they can evaluate the
risks differently, as you know.

21:48.296 --> 21:49.586 align:middle
And that includes,
you know, the,

21:49.676 --> 21:50.816 align:middle
the risk of higher inflation,

21:50.816 --> 21:52.146 align:middle
the risk it'll be
more persistent,

21:52.146 --> 21:54.086 align:middle
the risk that the labor market
will weaken-people are going

21:54.086 --> 21:56.616 align:middle
to have different
assessments of that risk.

21:56.986 --> 21:58.556 align:middle
So you put that in there, too.

21:59.126 --> 22:03.376 align:middle
So those are the two ways that,
that-the two things, I think,

22:03.376 --> 22:04.416 align:middle
that drive these things.

22:05.056 --> 22:07.896 align:middle
Remember, though, with-as
I mentioned earlier-with

22:08.296 --> 22:10.296 align:middle
uncertainty as elevated
as it is,

22:10.536 --> 22:15.006 align:middle
no one holds these rate paths
with a lot of conviction.

22:15.186 --> 22:17.766 align:middle
So that's really where it is.

22:17.766 --> 22:20.356 align:middle
It's a function of those things,
and I think as the data come in,

22:20.356 --> 22:22.976 align:middle
you should see those
differences diminish.

22:22.976 --> 22:23.576 align:middle
NICK TIMIRAOS.

22:23.576 --> 22:26.316 align:middle
If I could follow up-you've said
the policy is in a good place,

22:26.316 --> 22:27.656 align:middle
and that it's modestly
restrictive.

22:28.176 --> 22:30.816 align:middle
Given all the uncertainty-you
just talked about tariff levels,

22:30.816 --> 22:32.796 align:middle
uncertainty around the
pass-through-is it price

22:32.796 --> 22:35.176 align:middle
increases versus
margin compression?-some

22:35.236 --> 22:36.656 align:middle
of the softness that
Chris talked

22:36.656 --> 22:37.966 align:middle
about in, in labor and housing.

22:38.366 --> 22:40.316 align:middle
Why wouldn't it be
better to have rates

22:40.316 --> 22:44.466 align:middle
at a more neutral setting as the
economy heads into this period

22:44.466 --> 22:45.856 align:middle
of very high uncertainty?

22:45.856 --> 22:46.166 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

22:46.226 --> 22:49.406 align:middle
So if you just look
backward at the data, that,

22:49.476 --> 22:52.196 align:middle
that's what you would say,
but that's not-we have

22:52.196 --> 22:53.106 align:middle
to be forward looking.

22:53.516 --> 22:55.966 align:middle
And the thing that every
forecaster-every outside

22:55.966 --> 22:58.486 align:middle
forecaster and the
Fed is saying is

22:58.486 --> 23:01.506 align:middle
that we expect a meaningful
amount of inflation to arrive

23:01.506 --> 23:04.246 align:middle
in coming months, and we have
to take that into account.

23:04.246 --> 23:06.566 align:middle
So I think a backward-looking
look would,

23:06.686 --> 23:08.136 align:middle
would lead you to
a neutral stance.

23:08.136 --> 23:11.206 align:middle
But we, we can't-we have
to-we have to look at that.

23:11.206 --> 23:15.086 align:middle
And because the economy is still
solid, we can take the time

23:15.086 --> 23:16.766 align:middle
to actually see what's
going to happen.

23:16.766 --> 23:20.116 align:middle
It, it's, you know, the-there's
a range of possibilities on how,

23:20.266 --> 23:22.546 align:middle
how large the, the
inflation effects

23:22.546 --> 23:23.936 align:middle
and the other effects
are going to be.

23:23.936 --> 23:26.116 align:middle
So we'll make smarter
and better decisions

23:26.626 --> 23:29.466 align:middle
if we just wait a couple of
months or however long it takes

23:29.466 --> 23:32.376 align:middle
to get a sense of, of
really what, what is going

23:32.376 --> 23:34.106 align:middle
to be the pass-through
of inflation

23:34.106 --> 23:36.316 align:middle
and what're-what's going to
be the effects on spending

23:36.316 --> 23:38.026 align:middle
and on hiring and
all those things.

23:38.466 --> 23:39.566 align:middle
MICHELLE SMITH.

23:39.846 --> 23:40.306 align:middle
Mr. McKee.

23:41.596 --> 23:42.226 align:middle
MICHAEL MCKEE.

23:42.896 --> 23:45.006 align:middle
Michael McKee from Bloomberg
Radio and Television.

23:45.306 --> 23:48.826 align:middle
Your friend down at 1600
Pennsylvania Avenue continues

23:48.826 --> 23:51.186 align:middle
to lob insults in
your direction.

23:51.266 --> 23:52.926 align:middle
And I'm wondering, given now

23:52.926 --> 23:56.126 align:middle
that the Supreme Court has maybe
carved out the Fed from some

23:56.126 --> 24:01.546 align:middle
of the legal implications of
that, whether this is just noise

24:01.646 --> 24:05.656 align:middle
that the markets and everybody
should ignore until your term is

24:05.656 --> 24:10.686 align:middle
up or whether you worry that
it could lead to more pressure

24:10.686 --> 24:15.646 align:middle
on confidence on Wall Street-on
consumers-about the outlook

24:15.646 --> 24:16.276 align:middle
for the economy.

24:17.646 --> 24:18.186 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

24:18.416 --> 24:21.426 align:middle
Okay, from my standpoint,
it's-it's not complicated.

24:21.426 --> 24:24.236 align:middle
What everyone on the
FOMC wants is a good,

24:24.236 --> 24:25.656 align:middle
solid American economy

24:26.076 --> 24:29.006 align:middle
with a strong labor market
and, and price stability.

24:29.006 --> 24:29.666 align:middle
That's what we want.

24:29.756 --> 24:32.786 align:middle
We think our policy is well
positioned to-right now to,

24:32.836 --> 24:36.276 align:middle
to deliver that and, and to be
able to respond in a timely way

24:36.276 --> 24:37.376 align:middle
as the data lead us around.

24:37.846 --> 24:41.136 align:middle
The economy's been resilient,
and part of that is our stance,

24:41.706 --> 24:45.126 align:middle
and, again, we think we're-we're
in a good place on that

24:45.126 --> 24:47.146 align:middle
to respond to significant
economic developments.

24:47.636 --> 24:48.476 align:middle
That's what matters.

24:48.696 --> 24:50.916 align:middle
That is what matters
to us-pretty much,

24:50.916 --> 24:52.266 align:middle
that's all that matters to us.

24:52.716 --> 24:53.146 align:middle
MICHAEL MCKEE.

24:53.586 --> 24:57.286 align:middle
I need to ask-assuming you are
not reappointed, would you stay

24:57.286 --> 24:59.516 align:middle
on as Governor when
your term as Chair ends?

24:59.516 --> 24:59.736 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

24:59.736 --> 25:00.786 align:middle
I'm-I'm not thinking about that.

25:00.836 --> 25:01.796 align:middle
I'm thinking about this.

25:03.836 --> 25:04.246 align:middle
MICHELLE SMITH.

25:05.656 --> 25:07.596 align:middle
Andrew. ANDREW ACKERMAN.

25:07.596 --> 25:09.666 align:middle
Thanks, Mr. Chairman.

25:10.156 --> 25:13.196 align:middle
I guess with workplace
raids increasing,

25:13.226 --> 25:15.416 align:middle
picking up significantly-what
kind of effect would that have

25:15.416 --> 25:17.076 align:middle
on the labor market
in the short term?

25:17.076 --> 25:17.806 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

25:17.806 --> 25:19.486 align:middle
Sorry, sorry-with
what picking up?

25:19.776 --> 25:20.476 align:middle
ANDREW ACKERMAN.

25:20.546 --> 25:20.976 align:middle
Workplace raids.

25:20.976 --> 25:21.356 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

25:21.356 --> 25:24.086 align:middle
Ah, ah, immigration-so
you're asking immigration?

25:24.236 --> 25:24.776 align:middle
ANDREW ACKERMAN.

25:24.776 --> 25:25.366 align:middle
Yeah. CHAIR POWELL.

25:25.366 --> 25:26.466 align:middle
Yeah, you know-I, I
wouldn't want to speculate.

25:26.586 --> 25:29.466 align:middle
What-one way to get at that
from an economic standpoint-we,

25:29.466 --> 25:31.666 align:middle
of course, don't comment
on immigration policy.

25:31.666 --> 25:33.166 align:middle
It's not ours to
make or comment on.

25:33.646 --> 25:36.576 align:middle
But what you see is a-an,
an unemployment rate

25:36.616 --> 25:38.046 align:middle
that has been really solid

25:38.226 --> 25:41.226 align:middle
and at a low level-not
really increasing.

25:41.226 --> 25:44.026 align:middle
It's been in a-in a good range
and well within the range

25:44.026 --> 25:46.196 align:middle
of mainstream estimates
of maximum employment.

25:46.566 --> 25:49.136 align:middle
And that means, like, part
of that is that labor demand

25:49.136 --> 25:52.976 align:middle
and labor supply are kind of
moving down at the same rate.

25:53.056 --> 25:54.896 align:middle
Labor demand is, is
softening-you see

25:54.896 --> 25:57.136 align:middle
that in job creation-but
it's still kind

25:57.136 --> 25:57.976 align:middle
of at a healthy level.

25:58.276 --> 26:01.766 align:middle
And labor supply is, is
diminishing because the,

26:01.766 --> 26:04.656 align:middle
the immigration numbers
that we see are,

26:04.706 --> 26:05.916 align:middle
are much lower than they were.

26:05.916 --> 26:07.586 align:middle
So the-those two factors,

26:07.586 --> 26:09.596 align:middle
supply and demand-that's
what has kept the,

26:09.596 --> 26:12.816 align:middle
the unemployment
rate in a reasonably,

26:13.006 --> 26:13.956 align:middle
you know, stable place.

26:14.406 --> 26:14.796 align:middle
ANDREW ACKERMAN.

26:14.886 --> 26:15.276 align:middle
Okay, thanks.

26:15.276 --> 26:16.886 align:middle
The other thing I
wanted to follow up on is

26:16.886 --> 26:18.436 align:middle
if you could-if you
could elaborate

26:18.436 --> 26:21.566 align:middle
on the potential changes to the
SEP that you suggested were part

26:21.566 --> 26:22.866 align:middle
of the framework
review, I think.

26:23.986 --> 26:24.196 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

26:24.196 --> 26:27.766 align:middle
So the framework review
really has two tracks, right?

26:27.766 --> 26:30.556 align:middle
The first track is our-is
our policy framework.

26:30.646 --> 26:33.056 align:middle
That, that is reflected in
the consensus statement.

26:33.676 --> 26:36.576 align:middle
And we, we've said that we would
finish that and announce it

26:36.636 --> 26:37.616 align:middle
by the end of the summer.

26:38.196 --> 26:40.566 align:middle
So we're well along
in that process.

26:40.756 --> 26:43.066 align:middle
We've had the meetings
that we need to have,

26:43.066 --> 26:44.386 align:middle
and we're now going to be going

26:44.386 --> 26:48.756 align:middle
into [cough]-pardon me-into
discussions about, you know,

26:48.836 --> 26:50.526 align:middle
specific changes to language.

26:50.566 --> 26:53.576 align:middle
So that's-that's the
framework part of it.

26:53.966 --> 26:56.346 align:middle
The second part of it is
our communications tools

26:56.546 --> 26:59.606 align:middle
and practices [cough]-pardon
me-and that,

26:59.746 --> 27:01.966 align:middle
that part comes next, okay?

27:01.966 --> 27:03.776 align:middle
That's what we're going to
do in the meetings this fall.

27:04.216 --> 27:06.586 align:middle
Actually, what we did at
this meeting, though, is we,

27:06.776 --> 27:08.896 align:middle
we sort of prepared
the ground for that.

27:08.896 --> 27:11.616 align:middle
We had a-we had a meeting
where we talked at a high level

27:11.616 --> 27:12.846 align:middle
about a number of ideas.

27:13.166 --> 27:17.716 align:middle
The SEP is part of it-you know,
other-many, many other ideas.

27:17.716 --> 27:20.106 align:middle
It's-it's sort of-how do we
think our communications can

27:20.106 --> 27:20.786 align:middle
be improved?

27:21.126 --> 27:22.186 align:middle
There are a number of ideas.

27:22.186 --> 27:25.296 align:middle
People offered a really-it
was a great conversation.

27:25.606 --> 27:27.586 align:middle
Number of ideas-but we're-we're
going to look at those

27:27.586 --> 27:31.186 align:middle
with staff briefing and a
lot of thought in the fall.

27:31.186 --> 27:33.836 align:middle
And I would say, when it comes
to changing communications,

27:34.486 --> 27:37.826 align:middle
you know, I would only do-I
would only support things really

27:37.826 --> 27:40.496 align:middle
that only-implement things
that have very broad support.

27:40.876 --> 27:42.316 align:middle
And also, you want
to be really careful,

27:42.316 --> 27:44.676 align:middle
because I think our
communications are pretty

27:44.676 --> 27:45.286 align:middle
well received.

27:45.286 --> 27:49.496 align:middle
They're not broken, so more
is not necessarily better-but

27:49.626 --> 27:50.336 align:middle
better is better.

27:50.336 --> 27:53.146 align:middle
So we're going to be looking
at ways to do-to do things

27:53.146 --> 27:55.476 align:middle
that will improve the
clarity of what we do

27:55.476 --> 27:57.766 align:middle
for the benefit of,
of the public.

27:58.146 --> 27:58.666 align:middle
ANDREW ACKERMAN.

27:58.816 --> 28:00.636 align:middle
Thanks. MICHELLE SMITH.

28:00.696 --> 28:01.396 align:middle
Kelly. KELLY O'GRADY.

28:01.666 --> 28:02.506 align:middle
Thanks, Chair Powell.

28:02.506 --> 28:04.126 align:middle
Kelly O'Grady, CBS News.

28:04.616 --> 28:07.786 align:middle
You're famously known as the
guy that makes decision based

28:07.786 --> 28:10.126 align:middle
on data instead of speculation.

28:10.296 --> 28:12.276 align:middle
You've said today
in-the inflation data is

28:12.276 --> 28:14.616 align:middle
in a good place-we don't
know how tariffs are going

28:14.616 --> 28:17.316 align:middle
to impact prices going
forward-that's uncertain.

28:17.756 --> 28:19.676 align:middle
But I've got to go back to the
last time that you cut rates

28:19.756 --> 28:23.196 align:middle
in December, and there was
still the what-if of tariffs.

28:23.606 --> 28:27.546 align:middle
So what made you feel
comfortable cutting then,

28:27.986 --> 28:29.576 align:middle
when inflation was higher

28:30.006 --> 28:32.156 align:middle
than where it is today-and
you didn't cut today?

28:33.036 --> 28:33.446 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

28:33.446 --> 28:36.336 align:middle
Well, the, the forecast
for inflation

28:36.446 --> 28:39.656 align:middle
in December was 2½ core PCE

28:40.446 --> 28:44.956 align:middle
for 2025-the forecast was
2½ percent-which is a good

28:44.956 --> 28:45.916 align:middle
inflation forecast.

28:46.746 --> 28:50.226 align:middle
I think what we've learned is
that-and this was long before we

28:50.226 --> 28:52.796 align:middle
had any idea of what the
actual policies would be.

28:53.556 --> 28:54.816 align:middle
We've learned the
tariffs are going

28:54.816 --> 28:56.326 align:middle
to be substantially larger than,

28:56.326 --> 28:58.376 align:middle
than forecasters
generally thought.

28:58.376 --> 28:59.606 align:middle
And, you know, we, we don't-our,

28:59.716 --> 29:03.256 align:middle
our forecasts are generally
not particularly different

29:03.256 --> 29:04.996 align:middle
from those of other, you know,

29:04.996 --> 29:07.666 align:middle
well-resourced forecasting
operations.

29:07.666 --> 29:10.856 align:middle
So what we learned-and
particularly in April-was

29:10.856 --> 29:13.856 align:middle
that substantially larger
tariffs were coming and that

29:13.856 --> 29:15.266 align:middle
that would mean higher
inflation.

29:16.046 --> 29:16.696 align:middle
That's what happened.

29:16.696 --> 29:20.186 align:middle
And so you-now you see-you
saw 2½ percent forecast

29:20.186 --> 29:20.716 align:middle
in December.

29:20.716 --> 29:24.876 align:middle
You saw 2.8 percent in March,
and you see 3.1 percent now.

29:25.016 --> 29:28.096 align:middle
So it's six-tenths
higher inflation for 2025,

29:28.096 --> 29:30.726 align:middle
and that's-that's a big
part of, of the change.

29:30.726 --> 29:34.706 align:middle
And that's-that's due to the
effects of, of the tariffs

29:34.706 --> 29:36.446 align:middle
that are-you know, we, we
don't know where they're going

29:36.446 --> 29:38.056 align:middle
to land, but it's pretty
apparent they're going

29:38.056 --> 29:42.256 align:middle
to land higher than outside
forecasters were really guessing

29:42.666 --> 29:44.026 align:middle
at the end of last year.

29:44.526 --> 29:44.916 align:middle
KELLY O'GRADY.

29:44.916 --> 29:47.296 align:middle
My follow-up to that-I think
consumers-right?-were looking

29:47.296 --> 29:50.946 align:middle
for relief on rates when it
comes to mortgages-car loans.

29:51.016 --> 29:53.806 align:middle
Small businesses want to take
out more manageable loans.

29:54.176 --> 29:55.646 align:middle
When you look at the
cumulative inflation

29:55.646 --> 29:58.136 align:middle
over the past five
years, prices have risen

29:58.196 --> 30:00.456 align:middle
over 20 percent-it's
been a rough road.

30:00.926 --> 30:02.886 align:middle
So what is the tipping
point, then,

30:02.956 --> 30:06.346 align:middle
for the wait-and-see approach
in terms of how much it's going

30:06.346 --> 30:08.876 align:middle
to help versus when it
hurts the American consumer?

30:08.876 --> 30:10.376 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

30:10.376 --> 30:11.346 align:middle
Well, I mean, we're trying

30:11.346 --> 30:13.996 align:middle
to restore price-the best thing
we can do for the-for the public

30:14.046 --> 30:16.046 align:middle
that we serve is
restore price stability.

30:16.546 --> 30:18.986 align:middle
If we can, and we will,
restore price stability,

30:18.986 --> 30:23.486 align:middle
meaning 2 percent inflation on a
durable, sustainable basis-that

30:23.766 --> 30:26.926 align:middle
and also maximum employment-and
if we, we restore those things,

30:27.266 --> 30:28.106 align:middle
that's the best thing.

30:28.106 --> 30:29.036 align:middle
And that is our goal.

30:29.036 --> 30:31.006 align:middle
The best thing we can do
for the American people-for,

30:31.006 --> 30:32.286 align:middle
for households

30:32.286 --> 30:34.696 align:middle
and businesses-that
is the ultimate thing

30:34.696 --> 30:35.556 align:middle
that we can deliver.

30:36.106 --> 30:38.346 align:middle
And they can make their
decisions without having

30:38.346 --> 30:39.736 align:middle
to think about inflation
all the time.

30:40.076 --> 30:42.246 align:middle
So, in the meantime, we
have to keep rates high

30:42.636 --> 30:44.916 align:middle
to keep-to get inflation
all the way down.

30:45.266 --> 30:47.486 align:middle
They're not very
high-let's be honest.

30:47.486 --> 30:49.876 align:middle
I would say policy is modestly

30:50.066 --> 30:52.646 align:middle
or moderately-probably
modestly now-restrictive.

30:52.646 --> 30:55.126 align:middle
If you look at the
economy, it's not performing

30:55.126 --> 30:56.226 align:middle
as though it were performing

30:56.226 --> 30:59.056 align:middle
under very strict monetary
policy-very restrictive

30:59.056 --> 30:59.836 align:middle
monetary policy.

31:00.176 --> 31:02.756 align:middle
So I would say probably
modestly restrictive-and

31:02.756 --> 31:04.636 align:middle
so what it will take
is, is confidence

31:04.636 --> 31:06.156 align:middle
that inflation is coming down.

31:06.426 --> 31:08.346 align:middle
Now, I would say,
without tariffs,

31:08.706 --> 31:10.036 align:middle
that confidence would
be building

31:10.036 --> 31:12.726 align:middle
because if you-if you
see what's happening

31:12.726 --> 31:15.596 align:middle
with nonhousing services
and housing services,

31:15.596 --> 31:18.246 align:middle
which are the other two big
pieces other than goods,

31:18.666 --> 31:20.306 align:middle
those are coming down
really nicely now.

31:20.306 --> 31:22.426 align:middle
So I think we have to
learn a little more about,

31:22.426 --> 31:22.996 align:middle
about tariffs.

31:22.996 --> 31:24.586 align:middle
I don't know-I don't
know what the right way

31:24.586 --> 31:25.686 align:middle
for us to react will be.

31:26.126 --> 31:27.216 align:middle
I think it's hard to know with,

31:27.276 --> 31:29.776 align:middle
with any confidence how we
should react until we see,

31:30.136 --> 31:33.416 align:middle
really, the size of the
effects-then we can start

31:33.416 --> 31:34.406 align:middle
to make a better judgment.

31:34.406 --> 31:35.686 align:middle
So that's what we're doing,

31:35.686 --> 31:38.276 align:middle
and I think we can-we can
take the time to do that,

31:38.336 --> 31:40.726 align:middle
because unemployment
is 4.2 percent.

31:41.156 --> 31:42.186 align:middle
Wages are moving up.

31:42.236 --> 31:44.666 align:middle
Real wages are moving up
at a-at a healthy clip now.

31:45.126 --> 31:48.436 align:middle
And inflation is, you know-2.3
percent headline inflation

31:48.796 --> 31:49.976 align:middle
over a 12-month basis.

31:49.976 --> 31:51.516 align:middle
So it's a good economy,

31:51.726 --> 31:53.646 align:middle
and a solid economy
with decent growth.

31:54.466 --> 31:55.246 align:middle
MICHELLE SMITH.

31:55.786 --> 31:58.586 align:middle
Edward. EDWARD LAWRENCE.

31:58.586 --> 31:59.876 align:middle
Thank you, Chair Powell.

31:59.876 --> 32:02.246 align:middle
So you're saying that
uncertainty has come down,

32:02.306 --> 32:05.106 align:middle
the economy is moving at a
solid pace, inflation has come

32:05.106 --> 32:08.026 align:middle
down over the past three
months, and this is all moving

32:08.026 --> 32:08.766 align:middle
in the right direction.

32:08.766 --> 32:10.466 align:middle
So are you indicating here

32:10.466 --> 32:13.536 align:middle
that Americans should expect
some sort of economic pain

32:13.536 --> 32:14.896 align:middle
in the second half of the year?

32:14.896 --> 32:16.136 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

32:16.136 --> 32:17.656 align:middle
I'm not-I'm not saying
that at all.

32:17.856 --> 32:21.256 align:middle
You know, from our standpoint,
what I can say is that the,

32:21.356 --> 32:22.946 align:middle
the U.S. economy
is in solid shape.

32:23.046 --> 32:24.156 align:middle
Inflation has come down.

32:24.506 --> 32:26.576 align:middle
The unemployment rate
remains at 4.2 percent.

32:26.896 --> 32:28.656 align:middle
As I mentioned, real
wages are moving up.

32:28.656 --> 32:32.036 align:middle
It's a-it's a good-job
creation is at a healthy level.

32:32.466 --> 32:33.656 align:middle
Unemployment, again, as I said,

32:33.656 --> 32:35.706 align:middle
low-labor force participation
at a good place.

32:35.706 --> 32:37.756 align:middle
What we're waiting for
to reduce rates is,

32:37.896 --> 32:40.716 align:middle
is to understand what
will happen with, with,

32:40.716 --> 32:41.976 align:middle
really, the tariff inflation.

32:42.176 --> 32:45.586 align:middle
And there's a lot of
uncertainty about that.

32:46.106 --> 32:47.966 align:middle
Every forecaster
you can name who,

32:47.966 --> 32:51.836 align:middle
you know-who is a professional,
you know, forecaster with,

32:52.036 --> 32:53.996 align:middle
with adequate resources
and forecasts

32:53.996 --> 32:56.046 align:middle
for a living is forecasting,
you know,

32:56.046 --> 32:57.016 align:middle
a pretty significant-everyone

32:57.016 --> 33:00.286 align:middle
that I know is forecasting a
meaningful increase in inflation

33:00.766 --> 33:04.086 align:middle
in coming months from
tariffs because someone has

33:04.086 --> 33:05.056 align:middle
to pay for the tariffs.

33:05.156 --> 33:07.696 align:middle
And it will be someone in
that chain that I mentioned:

33:07.776 --> 33:11.306 align:middle
Between the manufacturer,
the exporter, the importer,

33:11.306 --> 33:14.336 align:middle
the retailer-ultimately,
somebody putting it into a,

33:14.416 --> 33:17.266 align:middle
a good of some kind or just
the consumer buying it-and,

33:17.406 --> 33:21.126 align:middle
you know, all through that, that
chain, people will be trying not

33:21.126 --> 33:22.706 align:middle
to be the ones who,
who pick up the cost.

33:22.706 --> 33:25.716 align:middle
But, ultimately, the cost of
the tariff has to be paid.

33:26.046 --> 33:28.536 align:middle
And some of it will fall
on the end consumer.

33:28.536 --> 33:31.556 align:middle
We know that because that's what
businesses say-that's what the,

33:31.686 --> 33:34.596 align:middle
the data say from past-so
we know that's coming.

33:35.156 --> 33:38.476 align:middle
And we just want to
see, see a little bit

33:38.476 --> 33:40.426 align:middle
of that before we make
judgments prematurely.

33:40.456 --> 33:40.826 align:middle
EDWARD LAWRENCE.

33:40.826 --> 33:42.766 align:middle
And follow-up on that-so
you've spent years, though,

33:42.766 --> 33:44.546 align:middle
talking about how, how
you're data dependent,

33:44.546 --> 33:46.506 align:middle
and be a little more
direct on this.

33:46.506 --> 33:48.226 align:middle
You know, now you're
making decisions looking

33:48.226 --> 33:51.766 align:middle
forward-doesn't the data you're
seeing today indicate there

33:51.766 --> 33:52.516 align:middle
should be a rate cut?

33:52.516 --> 33:53.266 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

33:53.366 --> 33:57.366 align:middle
No, I mean, you're-monetary
policy has

33:57.366 --> 33:58.246 align:middle
to be forward looking.

33:58.356 --> 33:59.286 align:middle
That is elementary.

33:59.426 --> 34:01.286 align:middle
You've got to be looking-I
always-we always talk

34:01.286 --> 34:04.246 align:middle
about the incoming data,
the evolving outlook,

34:04.396 --> 34:05.426 align:middle
and the balance of risks.

34:05.716 --> 34:08.096 align:middle
We say that over and over
and over again, right?

34:08.166 --> 34:10.556 align:middle
So it's always forward looking.

34:10.556 --> 34:13.286 align:middle
You know, if you know-at the
very beginning of the pandemic,

34:14.086 --> 34:16.486 align:middle
you know, we cut rates
to zero immediately.

34:16.486 --> 34:17.236 align:middle
Nothing had happened.

34:17.596 --> 34:19.906 align:middle
We just knew that it was
going to be really bad, right?

34:19.986 --> 34:23.696 align:middle
So we took very aggressive
forward looking-because we knew

34:23.696 --> 34:26.736 align:middle
things were going to
be unusually difficult.

34:27.246 --> 34:30.026 align:middle
So, of course, this,
this is something we,

34:30.116 --> 34:32.566 align:middle
we sort of know is coming-we
just don't know the size of it.

34:33.066 --> 34:36.076 align:middle
And, again, the economy
seems to be in solid shape.

34:36.246 --> 34:39.866 align:middle
So the labor market's not
crying out for a rate cut.

34:39.926 --> 34:43.536 align:middle
Businesses, you know, were in
a bit of shock after April 2.

34:43.536 --> 34:45.026 align:middle
But you see business
sentiment-you talk

34:45.026 --> 34:47.346 align:middle
to business people now-there's
a very different feeling now

34:47.346 --> 34:49.006 align:middle
that people are working
their way through this.

34:49.576 --> 34:51.426 align:middle
And they, they understand
how they're going to go,

34:51.426 --> 34:54.456 align:middle
and it's-it, it feels much
more positive and constructive

34:55.106 --> 34:56.786 align:middle
than it did three
months ago, let's say.

34:57.006 --> 34:59.656 align:middle
So, again, we think
that our current stance

34:59.656 --> 35:01.296 align:middle
of monetary policy
is in a good place.

35:01.746 --> 35:02.746 align:middle
MICHELLE SMITH.

35:03.296 --> 35:05.106 align:middle
Amara. AMARA OMEOKWE.

35:06.676 --> 35:06.936 align:middle
Thank you.

35:06.936 --> 35:08.186 align:middle
Amara Omeokwe with Bloomberg.

35:08.536 --> 35:10.996 align:middle
Chair Powell, in February, you
told Congress that the Fed is

35:10.996 --> 35:13.406 align:middle
"overworked, maybe,
not overstaffed."

35:13.836 --> 35:15.466 align:middle
Then, in a memo to-a
memo to staff

35:15.466 --> 35:18.236 align:middle
in May announcing a deferred
resignation program at the Fed,

35:18.576 --> 35:21.396 align:middle
you said you wanted to ensure
that the Fed was "right-sized."

35:21.966 --> 35:24.286 align:middle
Those two statements appear to
be at odds with one another.

35:24.656 --> 35:27.256 align:middle
Could you explain what changed
in the roughly three months

35:27.336 --> 35:29.396 align:middle
between those statements
that made you decide

35:29.396 --> 35:31.396 align:middle
that staff levels at
the Fed should decline?

35:31.696 --> 35:32.206 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

35:32.246 --> 35:34.246 align:middle
I don't see them at
all as, as in tension.

35:34.246 --> 35:37.336 align:middle
You know, so I was asked,
"Is the Fed overstaffed?"

35:37.596 --> 35:38.296 align:middle
And I said, "No."

35:38.646 --> 35:40.296 align:middle
You know, and I sort
of said as a pun,

35:40.296 --> 35:41.726 align:middle
"Overworked, but
not overstaffed."

35:41.786 --> 35:44.336 align:middle
People do work extremely
hard at the Fed,

35:44.336 --> 35:46.356 align:middle
and I know they work
hard at Bloomberg, too.

35:46.906 --> 35:48.246 align:middle
[Laughter] So-but we do.

35:48.246 --> 35:48.866 align:middle
We work hard.

35:49.206 --> 35:53.316 align:middle
But I would say this-so
we are careful stewards

35:53.316 --> 35:56.486 align:middle
of public resources, and
sometimes you need to show that.

35:56.806 --> 35:57.966 align:middle
So there've been several times

35:57.966 --> 36:00.196 align:middle
in our history-modern
history-where the Fed has said,

36:00.196 --> 36:00.586 align:middle
"You know what?

36:00.586 --> 36:01.936 align:middle
We're going to do a buyout.

36:01.936 --> 36:03.556 align:middle
We're going to-going
to show the public.

36:03.556 --> 36:04.596 align:middle
We're going to demonstrate

36:04.786 --> 36:07.716 align:middle
that we are good stewards
of public resources."

36:07.716 --> 36:09.496 align:middle
So we thought-and I,

36:09.686 --> 36:12.266 align:middle
I thought-that this
is a time when we can.

36:12.266 --> 36:14.316 align:middle
You know, we've-we grow
about-our headcount has grown

36:14.316 --> 36:15.696 align:middle
at about 1 percent a year.

36:16.336 --> 36:18.536 align:middle
So over the course
of a couple of years,

36:19.016 --> 36:21.536 align:middle
we're going to-we're doing
a careful scrub of the Board

36:21.536 --> 36:23.046 align:middle
and all of the Reserve
Banks, and we're going

36:23.046 --> 36:27.956 align:middle
to find 10 percent of employees
who can do something else.

36:28.026 --> 36:31.616 align:middle
Where, where we can-we can
streamline our operations-and

36:31.926 --> 36:34.626 align:middle
we, we think we can get there
in a year, in a couple of years.

36:34.626 --> 36:35.526 align:middle
We think we can do that.

36:35.956 --> 36:38.876 align:middle
And we think the-we
think the, the-this is,

36:38.876 --> 36:42.486 align:middle
this is without taking
risk to carrying

36:42.486 --> 36:43.866 align:middle
out our critical missions.

36:44.236 --> 36:47.036 align:middle
So this is something you do
very carefully-thoughtfully.

36:47.556 --> 36:49.826 align:middle
And you do it, again, respecting

36:49.826 --> 36:51.616 align:middle
that we have critical
missions to carry out.

36:51.676 --> 36:55.606 align:middle
I've had experience-a lot of
experience-in my prior careers,

36:56.076 --> 36:59.416 align:middle
you know, with headcount
reductions and things like that,

36:59.416 --> 37:01.236 align:middle
and this is how you
do it professionally.

37:01.386 --> 37:03.326 align:middle
You do it
carefully-thoughtfully-with a

37:03.326 --> 37:06.086 align:middle
lot of planning, and you do
it over a period of time.

37:06.606 --> 37:08.746 align:middle
And I, I think it's-I
think the Fed will be fine.

37:08.816 --> 37:11.736 align:middle
I think no one will notice any
decline in our ability to carry

37:11.736 --> 37:14.186 align:middle
out our missions, and I
think it's just us wanting

37:14.186 --> 37:15.336 align:middle
to demonstrate to the public

37:15.376 --> 37:17.916 align:middle
that we are actually
good stewards

37:18.116 --> 37:20.176 align:middle
of their-of their resources.

37:20.646 --> 37:23.186 align:middle
We're-we're effectively
wiping out 10 years

37:23.186 --> 37:25.046 align:middle
of headcount growth with this.

37:25.296 --> 37:27.146 align:middle
So, I mean, we just-we
wanted to show that,

37:27.426 --> 37:29.376 align:middle
you know-that we're
good stewards.

37:29.726 --> 37:30.156 align:middle
AMARA OMEOKWE.

37:30.156 --> 37:32.606 align:middle
How is progress on reducing
the headcount going so far?

37:32.746 --> 37:33.866 align:middle
Are you on track
to meet the goal?

37:33.866 --> 37:34.076 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

37:34.076 --> 37:35.146 align:middle
We're just at the
very beginning.

37:35.176 --> 37:37.136 align:middle
As you know, we're
doing a buyout program.

37:37.136 --> 37:38.806 align:middle
We're going to-we're
going to hit that goal.

37:39.556 --> 37:41.236 align:middle
I think many organizations find

37:41.236 --> 37:43.366 align:middle
that they can-that
they can do this.

37:43.366 --> 37:44.976 align:middle
You don't want to do it
every year or anything,

37:44.976 --> 37:47.126 align:middle
but you can do it at intervals.

37:47.386 --> 37:51.006 align:middle
And you, you wind up not,
you know-not interfering

37:51.006 --> 37:54.056 align:middle
with your ability to
perform your jobs.

37:56.076 --> 37:58.286 align:middle
MICHELLE SMITH.

37:58.286 --> 37:59.596 align:middle
Claire. CLAIRE JONES.

37:59.596 --> 38:02.576 align:middle
Chair Powell, Claire Jones
from the Financial Times.

38:02.696 --> 38:03.746 align:middle
As you're no doubt aware,

38:03.746 --> 38:07.016 align:middle
the Senate Finance Committee
has tabled its version

38:07.016 --> 38:10.246 align:middle
of the reconciliation bill
this week, and I was wondering

38:10.246 --> 38:13.076 align:middle
if you could tell me a
little bit about the tenor

38:13.076 --> 38:16.186 align:middle
of the debate at the FOMC
over the past few days

38:16.186 --> 38:19.816 align:middle
on fiscal policy and
the degree to which

38:19.816 --> 38:23.806 align:middle
that influenced people's
projections for 2026 and beyond.

38:24.096 --> 38:24.406 align:middle
Thank you.

38:24.946 --> 38:25.416 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

38:25.526 --> 38:29.696 align:middle
Yeah, so, you know, we don't-we
don't sit around and debate

38:29.696 --> 38:32.746 align:middle
or really discuss-we,
we take fiscal policy

38:32.746 --> 38:33.986 align:middle
as fully exogenous.

38:34.836 --> 38:39.386 align:middle
And so we, we actually, you
know, really didn't talk about,

38:39.486 --> 38:43.236 align:middle
about the bill or
the contents of it.

38:43.236 --> 38:44.146 align:middle
It's still evolving.

38:44.506 --> 38:49.626 align:middle
You know, when, when it gets
close-closer-remember also we

38:49.626 --> 38:51.286 align:middle
have a very, very large economy,

38:51.286 --> 38:54.226 align:middle
and that the effects
will be at the margin.

38:54.226 --> 38:56.076 align:middle
And, you know, I, I expect

38:56.076 --> 38:58.476 align:middle
that they'll-they may
already be in-but they will be

38:58.476 --> 38:59.416 align:middle
in by the next meeting.

38:59.416 --> 39:00.196 align:middle
We'll make an estimate.

39:00.796 --> 39:03.226 align:middle
But it's not a major thing;
it's nothing that we discuss.

39:04.136 --> 39:06.106 align:middle
It, it may have been
mentioned a couple of times-but

39:06.206 --> 39:07.356 align:middle
as something that's coming in.

39:07.916 --> 39:11.406 align:middle
But I think the outcome is-you
know, we don't know the outcome

39:11.406 --> 39:12.936 align:middle
yet there-so, hard
to be real specific.

39:12.936 --> 39:13.546 align:middle
MICHELLE SMITH.

39:16.536 --> 39:18.636 align:middle
Neil. NEIL IRWIN.

39:19.356 --> 39:20.306 align:middle
Thanks, Chair Powell.

39:20.306 --> 39:21.396 align:middle
Neil Irwin with Axios.

39:21.756 --> 39:24.416 align:middle
There've been some cutbacks in
economic statistics collection

39:24.416 --> 39:26.896 align:middle
in the last few weeks-worries
that long-running problems

39:26.896 --> 39:29.326 align:middle
around funding and response
rates may be getting worse.

39:29.626 --> 39:31.776 align:middle
How much is this
concern on your radar?

39:31.776 --> 39:34.416 align:middle
How much confidence do you have
that the gauges you're watching

39:34.416 --> 39:36.216 align:middle
to assess the economy
are reliable right now?

39:36.726 --> 39:37.606 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

39:37.606 --> 39:39.556 align:middle
You know, two things-one,

39:39.826 --> 39:42.686 align:middle
the data we get right
now-we, we can do our jobs.

39:42.796 --> 39:44.816 align:middle
I'm not concerned that
we can't do our jobs.

39:44.906 --> 39:47.996 align:middle
That's not the-that's
not the point.

39:47.996 --> 39:50.196 align:middle
The point, really, is
that we are starting

39:50.196 --> 39:53.726 align:middle
to see, you know, layoffs.

39:53.726 --> 39:57.176 align:middle
And, and important
gatherers of data are saying

39:57.176 --> 39:58.676 align:middle
that they're-they're
having to cut back

39:58.676 --> 39:59.796 align:middle
on the size of their surveys.

40:00.086 --> 40:02.396 align:middle
That's going to lead to more
volatility in the surveys.

40:02.896 --> 40:04.316 align:middle
I think we should
take a step back.

40:04.446 --> 40:06.726 align:middle
And, you know, from our
standpoint-and I think the

40:06.726 --> 40:08.516 align:middle
standpoint of businesses
and governments

40:08.516 --> 40:12.536 align:middle
and everyone-having really good
data on the state of the economy

40:12.536 --> 40:14.686 align:middle
at any given time is
a huge public good.

40:14.686 --> 40:16.996 align:middle
It helps. It doesn't
just help the Fed.

40:16.996 --> 40:18.846 align:middle
It helps the government,
it helps Congress,

40:18.846 --> 40:19.936 align:middle
it helps the executive branch.

40:20.366 --> 40:22.156 align:middle
More importantly, really,
it helps businesses.

40:22.236 --> 40:24.126 align:middle
They need to know what's
going on in the economy.

40:24.446 --> 40:26.526 align:middle
The United States has
been a leader for many,

40:26.526 --> 40:29.966 align:middle
many years in this whole
project of measuring

40:29.966 --> 40:32.046 align:middle
and understanding
what's happening in,

40:32.046 --> 40:34.496 align:middle
in our very large
and dynamic economy.

40:34.866 --> 40:38.606 align:middle
And I hate to see-I hate to
see us cutting back on that

40:38.606 --> 40:42.626 align:middle
because it, it is a real
benefit to the general public

40:42.666 --> 40:46.546 align:middle
that people in all kinds of
jobs have the best possible

40:46.926 --> 40:49.136 align:middle
understanding of what's
happening in the economy and,

40:49.136 --> 40:50.506 align:middle
and, hence, what's
likely to happen.

40:50.816 --> 40:53.956 align:middle
It's very hard to measure what's
going on in the U.S. economy.

40:53.956 --> 40:55.886 align:middle
If, if you read-there
was a book called-well,

40:56.586 --> 41:00.106 align:middle
it's really remarkable
how many things you need

41:00.106 --> 41:02.606 align:middle
to understand to
estimate U.S. GDP.

41:02.606 --> 41:06.466 align:middle
Very, very difficult-and it's so
important that we get it right.

41:06.466 --> 41:10.246 align:middle
I just would-I just would say
it's not a place to-I would want

41:10.706 --> 41:13.396 align:middle
to keep investing in that,
you know, for the good

41:13.546 --> 41:14.466 align:middle
of the general public.

41:14.466 --> 41:15.066 align:middle
MICHELLE SMITH.

41:16.536 --> 41:18.166 align:middle
Victoria. VICTORIA GUIDA.

41:22.646 --> 41:24.996 align:middle
Hi. Victoria Guida
with Politico.

41:25.116 --> 41:28.136 align:middle
So you're conducting this
monetary policy strategy

41:28.456 --> 41:31.946 align:middle
framework review, but
next year we're supposed

41:31.946 --> 41:35.126 align:middle
to have a new Fed Chair, and
I'm wondering if that affects

41:35.126 --> 41:37.116 align:middle
at all the way that
you're approaching this.

41:37.286 --> 41:40.076 align:middle
How do you ensure that this
framework will actually

41:40.076 --> 41:40.646 align:middle
be durable?

41:41.606 --> 41:41.976 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

41:41.976 --> 41:45.776 align:middle
You know, the framework goes
back to-the framework document

41:45.776 --> 41:51.036 align:middle
goes back to 2012, and it's-it's
the Committee's document.

41:51.606 --> 41:52.576 align:middle
It's not like we're going

41:52.576 --> 41:54.126 align:middle
to invent a brand
new way to do things.

41:54.126 --> 41:57.576 align:middle
It's-it's been an evolving
document, so it shouldn't depend

41:57.576 --> 41:59.696 align:middle
on who the Chair is at all.

41:59.696 --> 42:01.556 align:middle
It should depend on what's
happening in the economy

42:01.556 --> 42:02.736 align:middle
and what the Committee
wants to do.

42:02.736 --> 42:07.316 align:middle
So, yeah, it isn't really tied
to any particular Chair, and,

42:07.316 --> 42:10.476 align:middle
you know, we used to
renew it every year.

42:10.476 --> 42:11.816 align:middle
Now we do it every five years.

42:11.816 --> 42:14.606 align:middle
So-but I, I don't think
anybody-I've never heard anyone

42:14.606 --> 42:18.316 align:middle
raise this issue that, you know,
a new Chair might want to come

42:18.316 --> 42:19.826 align:middle
in and go in a completely
different direction.

42:19.826 --> 42:21.346 align:middle
I really-I really don't
think that's right.

42:21.346 --> 42:23.956 align:middle
But, you know, that's not-not
going to be up to me to decide.

42:24.446 --> 42:24.856 align:middle
VICTORIA GUIDA.

42:24.856 --> 42:25.786 align:middle
Is that affecting at all

42:25.846 --> 42:27.646 align:middle
that you're-who you're
consulting with- CHAIR POWELL.

42:27.646 --> 42:28.286 align:middle
No, not at all.

42:28.286 --> 42:29.036 align:middle
Not in any way.

42:29.616 --> 42:30.286 align:middle
MICHELLE SMITH.

42:31.776 --> 42:37.246 align:middle
Matt. MATT EGAN.

42:37.246 --> 42:38.136 align:middle
Thank you, Chair Powell.

42:38.206 --> 42:40.276 align:middle
Matt Egan from CNN.

42:40.276 --> 42:44.046 align:middle
Relatively low gas prices
this year have helped drive

42:44.196 --> 42:46.756 align:middle
down inflation in
recent reports,

42:46.816 --> 42:48.386 align:middle
but that trend is
starting to reverse,

42:48.526 --> 42:50.156 align:middle
given the crisis
in the Middle East.

42:50.826 --> 42:54.166 align:middle
How are you thinking about how
the Israel-Iran conflict will

42:54.166 --> 42:56.956 align:middle
impact the economy,
especially inflation,

42:56.956 --> 43:01.356 align:middle
and what lessons were
learned during the 2022 period

43:01.816 --> 43:04.986 align:middle
when another conflict, the
Russia-Ukraine war, sent oil

43:04.986 --> 43:06.206 align:middle
and gas prices skyrocketing?

43:06.756 --> 43:07.026 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

43:07.226 --> 43:07.656 align:middle
So, of course,

43:07.656 --> 43:10.926 align:middle
we're watching-like everybody
else is-what's going on.

43:10.926 --> 43:12.696 align:middle
I really don't have
any comment on that.

43:12.746 --> 43:14.656 align:middle
You know, possible that,

43:14.656 --> 43:17.696 align:middle
that we'll see higher
energy prices-what's tended

43:17.696 --> 43:21.056 align:middle
to happen is, when there's
turmoil in the Middle East,

43:21.056 --> 43:24.766 align:middle
you may see a spike in energy
prices-but tends to come down.

43:25.146 --> 43:29.316 align:middle
Those things don't generally
tend to have lasting effects

43:29.316 --> 43:31.686 align:middle
on inflation, although,
of course, in the 1970s,

43:31.686 --> 43:33.956 align:middle
they famously did,
because you had a series

43:33.956 --> 43:36.166 align:middle
of very, very large shocks.

43:36.776 --> 43:39.676 align:middle
But we haven't seen anything
like that-that-like that now.

43:39.676 --> 43:42.656 align:middle
The U.S. economy is far less
dependent on foreign oil

43:43.376 --> 43:46.016 align:middle
than it was back in the 1970s.

43:46.016 --> 43:48.746 align:middle
So, but- MATT EGAN.

43:48.746 --> 43:50.416 align:middle
A quick follow-up-I've
just got to ask you

43:50.416 --> 43:51.946 align:middle
about artificial intelligence.

43:51.986 --> 43:55.126 align:middle
Some technology executives
have recently been warning

43:55.126 --> 43:59.536 align:middle
that AI could wipe out a large
chunk of entry-level jobs

43:59.536 --> 44:02.226 align:middle
and significantly increase
the unemployment rate.

44:02.636 --> 44:05.776 align:middle
I'm wondering how concerned you
are, if at all, about the threat

44:05.776 --> 44:08.186 align:middle
that AI poses to employment.

44:09.076 --> 44:09.716 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

44:09.816 --> 44:11.196 align:middle
So this is the question.

44:11.386 --> 44:15.726 align:middle
The question really is, will
AI be more augmenting labor

44:15.726 --> 44:17.036 align:middle
or replacing labor?

44:17.426 --> 44:20.066 align:middle
And I wouldn't-I, I-we all
see those announcements,

44:20.066 --> 44:20.956 align:middle
including one today.

44:21.236 --> 44:27.196 align:middle
I wouldn't overread a, a
couple of data points, because,

44:27.586 --> 44:30.136 align:middle
you know, AI should be
creating jobs at the same time.

44:30.266 --> 44:32.416 align:middle
It may be replacing-it
may be doing both.

44:33.016 --> 44:37.806 align:middle
Anyone who's done any
work with it-with AI-will,

44:37.876 --> 44:41.816 align:middle
will have been a little bit
stunned at how capable it is.

44:42.106 --> 44:43.616 align:middle
And it's just a different thing.

44:43.616 --> 44:45.526 align:middle
So I think this is something

44:45.526 --> 44:48.656 align:middle
that certainly has
transformational potential-and

44:48.656 --> 44:51.316 align:middle
probably we're in the
very early stages of it.

44:51.376 --> 44:54.116 align:middle
They say what you're seeing
now compared to what you'll see

44:54.116 --> 44:56.786 align:middle
in two years is, is going
to be very different

44:56.786 --> 44:57.636 align:middle
and even more effective.

44:57.636 --> 44:59.716 align:middle
So I think it's really
hard to know.

44:59.836 --> 45:01.716 align:middle
You know, of course, there
are optimists who feel

45:01.716 --> 45:03.956 align:middle
like it's going to make
everybody much more,

45:04.426 --> 45:06.606 align:middle
you know-much more productive.

45:06.636 --> 45:08.706 align:middle
And there are those
who think it's going

45:08.706 --> 45:10.396 align:middle
to replace an awful
lot of jobs right

45:10.396 --> 45:12.526 align:middle
across the income
spectrum-you know, white collar,

45:12.526 --> 45:13.556 align:middle
blue collar, and everything.

45:13.556 --> 45:15.206 align:middle
So I just don't know.

45:15.716 --> 45:17.796 align:middle
We don't-we don't have
a "house view" on that.

45:18.426 --> 45:19.786 align:middle
But this is-this is going

45:19.786 --> 45:22.236 align:middle
to be a very important
question for some time.

45:22.416 --> 45:22.906 align:middle
MICHELLE SMITH.

45:24.416 --> 45:28.826 align:middle
Greg. GREG ROBB.

45:30.726 --> 45:30.976 align:middle
Thank you.

45:31.156 --> 45:32.316 align:middle
Greg Robb from MarketWatch.

45:32.876 --> 45:34.706 align:middle
I was wondering if you could
step back a little bit,

45:34.706 --> 45:35.876 align:middle
Chair Powell.

45:36.606 --> 45:40.126 align:middle
You know, there's a spate of
articles and a lot of op-eds now

45:40.126 --> 45:43.016 align:middle
in the newspapers saying
that the U.S. economy

45:43.206 --> 45:46.926 align:middle
and the global economy is going
through this profound change,

45:46.926 --> 45:51.016 align:middle
you know, akin to the end of the
Bretton Woods era in the 1970s.

45:51.846 --> 45:55.926 align:middle
And don't you owe the American
people, like, some sort of,

45:55.926 --> 45:58.556 align:middle
like, explanation for
what we're going through?

45:59.046 --> 46:01.206 align:middle
I mean, I, I noticed
earlier this month when you,

46:01.256 --> 46:03.556 align:middle
you talked about Bretton Woods
a little bit, and you said

46:03.556 --> 46:06.276 align:middle
that Bretton Woods-the
Fed staff had to, like,

46:06.276 --> 46:08.406 align:middle
change how they've-the
dollar-movements

46:08.406 --> 46:10.066 align:middle
of the dollar was
impacting the economy.

46:10.506 --> 46:13.756 align:middle
Are we going through
something like that now-that,

46:13.756 --> 46:14.906 align:middle
you know-are you having

46:14.906 --> 46:16.876 align:middle
to change how you
do monetary policy?

46:16.956 --> 46:19.046 align:middle
Is it-is it that fundamental
of a change under way?

46:19.446 --> 46:20.956 align:middle
Thanks. CHAIR POWELL.

46:21.106 --> 46:24.336 align:middle
It's-it's certainly a time
of real change-you know,

46:24.336 --> 46:28.386 align:middle
from a geopolitical standpoint,
from a trade standpoint,

46:29.016 --> 46:31.946 align:middle
from an immigration
standpoint-you see this not just

46:31.946 --> 46:32.776 align:middle
here, but everywhere.

46:32.776 --> 46:34.916 align:middle
So there's-there's
quite a lot going on.

46:35.416 --> 46:37.496 align:middle
It doesn't change the
way we do monetary policy

46:37.496 --> 46:38.256 align:middle
in the near term.

46:38.256 --> 46:40.946 align:middle
I mean, but-and it doesn't
change our objectives

46:40.946 --> 46:42.056 align:middle
or what we need to do.

46:42.056 --> 46:43.056 align:middle
And, you know, these,

46:43.096 --> 46:45.456 align:middle
these things are not really our
issues-they're really issues

46:45.456 --> 46:46.846 align:middle
for elected governments.

46:46.846 --> 46:48.836 align:middle
All of those issues are really
for elected governments.

46:48.876 --> 46:52.256 align:middle
But there's no question
it's a time of, of real,

46:52.596 --> 46:56.086 align:middle
real change-and very hard
to see where that goes.

46:56.496 --> 47:00.076 align:middle
Will it be-there have been
many, many things written

47:00.076 --> 47:02.096 align:middle
about how it's going to be
a more inflationary time.

47:02.096 --> 47:04.246 align:middle
That's possible;
it's not guaranteed.

47:04.716 --> 47:06.836 align:middle
You know, AI could cut in
a very other direction.

47:07.156 --> 47:11.386 align:middle
AI could make people much
more productive and-and push

47:11.386 --> 47:12.026 align:middle
in the other direction.

47:12.026 --> 47:12.486 align:middle
I don't know, though.

47:12.486 --> 47:15.286 align:middle
So you're right, but,
honestly, our focus is,

47:15.346 --> 47:17.226 align:middle
is a much more practical
one, and that is,

47:17.596 --> 47:19.576 align:middle
how do we keep inflation
low and,

47:19.576 --> 47:22.216 align:middle
and employment high
in the near term?

47:22.216 --> 47:23.866 align:middle
That's really what we're about.

47:24.866 --> 47:25.176 align:middle
MICHELLE SMITH.

47:26.506 --> 47:28.506 align:middle
Mark. Mark Hamrick.

47:31.236 --> 47:31.666 align:middle
MARK HAMRICK.

47:31.666 --> 47:32.436 align:middle
Thank you.

47:33.726 --> 47:35.176 align:middle
Hello, Chairman Powell.

47:35.176 --> 47:36.486 align:middle
Mark Hamrick with Bankrate.

47:36.836 --> 47:39.396 align:middle
What is the view about the
growing amount of slack

47:39.396 --> 47:42.446 align:middle
in the job market, including
the softening in payrolls,

47:42.446 --> 47:45.496 align:middle
the forecast of a modest rise
in the unemployment rate,

47:46.036 --> 47:49.986 align:middle
and the ability of workers
to demand wage hikes or not

47:49.986 --> 47:52.836 align:middle
in this environment where
you have inflation surging?

47:52.836 --> 47:53.676 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

47:54.236 --> 47:56.896 align:middle
I don't, you don't see-you
don't really see unemployment

47:56.896 --> 47:57.316 align:middle
going up.

47:57.316 --> 47:59.726 align:middle
You don't see increased slack,
really-I mean, at the margin.

47:59.886 --> 48:02.126 align:middle
Remember, you're at 4.2
percent unemployment.

48:02.126 --> 48:04.096 align:middle
That, that was for many,

48:04.096 --> 48:06.576 align:middle
many years-that was an
extremely low level.

48:06.576 --> 48:08.836 align:middle
It happens to have come up
off of an even lower level.

48:09.186 --> 48:12.086 align:middle
As we came out of the pandemic,
we were as low as 3.4 percent.

48:12.126 --> 48:16.056 align:middle
But 4.2 percent is probably
at the low end of estimates

48:16.056 --> 48:20.166 align:middle
of the longer-run, you
know, sustainable level

48:20.256 --> 48:21.726 align:middle
of natural rate of unemployment.

48:22.096 --> 48:24.186 align:middle
So I wouldn't-I guess I
wouldn't agree with that.

48:24.186 --> 48:27.236 align:middle
And also, in terms
of wages-you know,

48:27.236 --> 48:31.496 align:middle
real wages after inflation have
been moving up sort of more

48:31.716 --> 48:34.326 align:middle
than was consistent with
2 percent inflation.

48:34.326 --> 48:38.776 align:middle
They're still moving up at a-at
a healthy clip-and I think much

48:38.776 --> 48:42.156 align:middle
more consistent with 2 percent
inflation, given, given a,

48:42.346 --> 48:45.596 align:middle
a reasonable assessment
of, of trend productivity.

48:46.006 --> 48:47.926 align:middle
So it's a pretty
good labor market.

48:47.926 --> 48:49.346 align:middle
You know, you're
right that the level

48:49.346 --> 48:51.826 align:middle
of job creation has come
down, but so has the supply

48:51.826 --> 48:55.186 align:middle
of workers-the change in the
supply-the, the new supply.

48:55.626 --> 48:58.106 align:middle
So you've seen-you've seen the
unemployment rate remain pretty

48:58.106 --> 48:59.306 align:middle
stable at 4.2.

48:59.946 --> 49:02.226 align:middle
It's been as high as
4.3, but, you know,

49:02.726 --> 49:03.896 align:middle
those are-those are
good numbers.

49:03.896 --> 49:05.056 align:middle
So it's a pretty
good labor market.

49:05.306 --> 49:07.676 align:middle
There's-the thing is, there's-a
more concerning thing is,

49:07.976 --> 49:09.486 align:middle
there's not-there are
not a lot of layoffs,

49:09.566 --> 49:12.206 align:middle
but there are not a lot
of, of job creation.

49:12.436 --> 49:13.786 align:middle
The number-if you're
out of work,

49:14.496 --> 49:16.116 align:middle
it's-it's hard to find a job.

49:16.276 --> 49:19.476 align:middle
But very few people are
being laid off at this point.

49:19.546 --> 49:24.396 align:middle
So that's-an equilibrium we
watch very, very carefully,

49:24.396 --> 49:27.996 align:middle
because if there were to be,
you know, significant layoffs

49:28.496 --> 49:30.606 align:middle
and the job-finding rate
were to remain this low,

49:30.606 --> 49:32.216 align:middle
you would have a lot-you
would have an increase

49:32.216 --> 49:33.536 align:middle
in unemployment fairly quickly.

49:33.776 --> 49:34.616 align:middle
But that hasn't happened.

49:34.786 --> 49:35.726 align:middle
It really hasn't happened.

49:35.726 --> 49:40.466 align:middle
We're-so the U.S. economy has
defied all kinds of forecasts

49:40.466 --> 49:44.026 align:middle
for it to, to weaken, really,
over the last three years,

49:44.626 --> 49:47.386 align:middle
and it's been remarkable
to see-just again and again

49:47.386 --> 49:48.916 align:middle
when people think it's
going to weaken out.

49:48.916 --> 49:51.686 align:middle
Eventually it will, but we
don't see signs of that now.

49:51.686 --> 49:52.386 align:middle
MICHELLE SMITH.

49:52.976 --> 49:54.966 align:middle
Go to Jean for the
last question.

49:54.966 --> 49:57.056 align:middle
JEAN YUNG.

49:57.426 --> 49:58.066 align:middle
Hi, Chair Powell.

49:58.096 --> 49:59.716 align:middle
Jean Yung with MNI Market News.

50:00.456 --> 50:02.456 align:middle
There's been a lot
of talk about cuts.

50:02.916 --> 50:05.956 align:middle
I wanted to ask you-why do you
think there are no forecasts

50:06.046 --> 50:10.356 align:middle
for rates to rise or, or even to
stay where they are next year,

50:10.806 --> 50:14.046 align:middle
given that the projection
for inflation is to rise

50:14.046 --> 50:18.246 align:middle
to 3 percent and there's a
lot of-there's some skepticism

50:18.246 --> 50:22.506 align:middle
over whether those price hikes
will be a one-time event?

50:22.506 --> 50:23.216 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

50:23.296 --> 50:25.836 align:middle
So there are a number of people
on the Committee who wrote

50:25.836 --> 50:28.286 align:middle
down no cuts this year
but some cuts next year.

50:28.286 --> 50:32.176 align:middle
So, look, I think, you
know, people are writing

50:32.176 --> 50:34.606 align:middle
down their most likely
path, right?

50:34.606 --> 50:36.716 align:middle
They're not saying
there's zero possibility

50:36.716 --> 50:37.906 align:middle
of other things, really.

50:37.906 --> 50:40.716 align:middle
Really, it's-think of it
as the least unlikely path

50:40.756 --> 50:42.896 align:middle
in a situation like this where
uncertainty is very high.

50:43.626 --> 50:47.656 align:middle
I, I think-again, people
write-they write down their,

50:47.766 --> 50:50.706 align:middle
their rate paths, and
they do not have, like,

50:50.706 --> 50:52.976 align:middle
a really high conviction that
this is exactly what's going

50:52.976 --> 50:54.096 align:middle
to happen over the
next two years.

50:54.096 --> 50:55.726 align:middle
No one feels that way
about their rate path.

50:55.776 --> 50:57.716 align:middle
They feel like, "What am
I going to write down?"

50:57.716 --> 50:58.756 align:middle
I mean, what would
you write down?

50:59.216 --> 51:02.336 align:middle
It's not easy to, to be-to
do that with confidence.

51:02.806 --> 51:04.326 align:middle
So I would just say it that way.

51:04.766 --> 51:07.676 align:middle
We don't rule things
in or out-certainly,

51:07.676 --> 51:09.896 align:middle
a hike is not the
base case at all.

51:09.896 --> 51:11.476 align:middle
It's not something
people are writing down.

51:12.066 --> 51:15.466 align:middle
But, in the meantime,
we do the best we can

51:15.466 --> 51:16.566 align:middle
with these forecasts, and I,

51:16.566 --> 51:18.966 align:middle
I think they're-they're
representative of, you know,

51:18.966 --> 51:21.666 align:middle
of the different forecasts and
different reaction functions

51:21.666 --> 51:22.576 align:middle
that people on the
Committee have.

51:23.356 --> 51:24.236 align:middle
So thank you very much.

51:27.126 --> 51:28.186 align:middle
Thanks.

